// ── Eyelid · Ch 1 · Facial Nerve (CN VII) Disorders & Palsy
const NEURO_LID1_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Contrast a central (upper motor neuron) and a peripheral (lower motor neuron) facial palsy.", answer: "Central/UMN palsy spares the forehead → CONTRALATERAL weakness of the LOWER two-thirds of the face with preserved orbicularis oculi and emotional expression (bilateral cortical input to the upper face). Peripheral/LMN palsy affects the whole side → IPSILATERAL upper AND lower facial weakness (including forehead and eye closure)." },
  { id: 2, question: "Besides motor function, what special (non-motor) functions does CN VII carry?", answer: "Parasympathetic lacrimation (lacrimal reflex), taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue (via chorda tympani), salivary secretion, and innervation of the stapedius muscle (dysacusis if involved)." },
  { id: 3, question: "How does a SUPRANUCLEAR facial palsy present?", answer: "Contralateral weakness of the lower two-thirds of the face WITHOUT orbicularis oculi weakness, and with retained involuntary (emotional) expression — because the upper face has bilateral cortical innervation and emotional movement uses separate extrapyramidal pathways." },
  { id: 4, question: "What accompanies a NUCLEAR facial palsy?", answer: "Facial monoplegia (e.g., congenital, as in Möbius syndrome) plus CN VI nucleus involvement (ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy, since CN VII loops around it) and frequent ataxia; occasionally Horner syndrome." },
  { id: 5, question: "What are the findings of a cerebellopontine angle (CPA) lesion affecting CN VII?", answer: "Decreased tearing, dysgeusia, loss of salivation, loss of taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, hearing impairment, nystagmus, vertigo, ataxia, and adjacent cranial nerve (V and VI) findings." },
  { id: 6, question: "How does Ramsay Hunt syndrome (geniculate ganglionitis) differ from a CPA lesion?", answer: "Ramsay Hunt (herpes zoster oticus, geniculate ganglion) has the SAME facial/taste/hearing findings as a CPA lesion, but the brainstem and other cranial nerves are NOT involved. It features a painful vesicular eruption of the ear/auditory canal." },
  { id: 7, question: "What does a lesion within the fallopian canal add to a facial palsy?", answer: "Involvement of the nerve to the stapedius (dysacusis/hyperacusis) and of the chorda tympani (loss of taste to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and impaired salivary secretion) — localizing the lesion proximal to these branches." },
  { id: 8, question: "What does a lesion distal to the chorda tympani (or distal to the stylomastoid foramen) cause?", answer: "Distal to chorda tympani: isolated paralysis of the facial muscles (taste/salivation spared). Distal to the stylomastoid foramen branching: only certain branches of CN VII are affected (localized facial weakness)." },
  { id: 9, question: "List causes of BILATERAL facial (CN VII) palsy.", answer: "Congenital Möbius syndrome, sarcoidosis, basilar meningitis (bacterial, viral, spirochetal), Guillain-Barré syndrome, and neurofibromatosis type 2 (bilateral acoustic neuromas)." },
  { id: 10, question: "Describe the recovery and prognosis of Bell palsy.", answer: "Recovery typically begins within 3 weeks of onset and is complete by 2–3 months. In ~15%, recovery is incomplete with significant synkinesis (co-contraction). Poor prognostic factors: complete palsy at presentation, impaired lacrimation, dysacusis, pregnancy, diabetes, and advanced age." },
  { id: 11, question: "What is the medical treatment of Bell palsy?", answer: "A 7–10-day course of oral corticosteroids started within the first 72 hours of onset (if no systemic contraindication). Antivirals (acyclovir, famciclovir, valacyclovir) have unclear/uncertain added benefit." },
  { id: 12, question: "How do you protect the cornea in a facial palsy with poor eyelid closure?", answer: "Mild: artificial tears/lubricants, taping the lid shut, moisture chambers. More: punctal plugs, tarsorrhaphy, botulinum toxin into the levator to induce protective ptosis, or eyelid-reanimation surgery / gold or platinum upper-lid weight implants. If CN V is also involved (neurotrophic keratitis), consider early tarsorrhaphy." },
  { id: 13, question: "Distinguish recurrent unilateral from progressive facial palsy by cause.", answer: "Recurrent unilateral: idiopathic, diabetes, Lyme disease, or Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome. Progressive facial palsy: neoplastic — tumor invasion (brainstem, CPA, parotid) or diffuse infiltration (e.g., meningeal carcinomatosis)." },
];

const NEURO_LID1_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "A patient with forehead-SPARING facial weakness on one side has a:", options: ["Peripheral (LMN) CN VII palsy", "Central (UMN) facial palsy", "Myasthenia", "Horner syndrome"], correct: 1, explanation: "Forehead sparing (bilateral cortical input to upper face) indicates a central/UMN lesion; the lower face is weak contralaterally." },
  { id: 2, question: "A peripheral (LMN) facial palsy causes:", options: ["Contralateral lower-face weakness only", "Ipsilateral weakness of the WHOLE side including forehead and eye closure", "No eyelid involvement", "Pupil dilation"], correct: 1, explanation: "An LMN CN VII lesion weakens the entire ipsilateral face, including the forehead and orbicularis oculi." },
  { id: 3, question: "Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue travels with CN VII via the:", options: ["Greater petrosal nerve", "Chorda tympani", "Stapedial nerve", "Vidian nerve"], correct: 1, explanation: "The chorda tympani carries anterior two-thirds taste (and parasympathetic salivary fibers)." },
  { id: 4, question: "Ramsay Hunt syndrome is facial palsy due to:", options: ["Herpes zoster of the geniculate ganglion (zoster oticus)", "A pontine stroke", "Sarcoidosis", "A parotid tumor"], correct: 0, explanation: "Ramsay Hunt = geniculate ganglionitis from herpes zoster, with ear vesicles; the brainstem/other CNs are spared." },
  { id: 5, question: "A facial palsy with ipsilateral gaze palsy and ataxia localizes to the:", options: ["Facial nucleus/pons (nuclear)", "Parotid gland", "Stylomastoid foramen", "Cortex"], correct: 0, explanation: "Nuclear CN VII lesions involve the adjacent CN VI nucleus (gaze palsy) and nearby cerebellar connections (ataxia)." },
  { id: 6, question: "Which combination suggests a cerebellopontine angle lesion?", options: ["Facial palsy + hearing loss + vertigo + CN V/VI signs", "Isolated lower-face weakness", "Pupil-involving CN III palsy", "Bitemporal hemianopia"], correct: 0, explanation: "CPA lesions affect CN VII and VIII with vertigo/hearing loss plus adjacent CN V and VI findings." },
  { id: 7, question: "Dysacusis (hyperacusis) in a facial palsy indicates involvement of the nerve to the:", options: ["Stapedius (fallopian canal)", "Levator", "Lateral rectus", "Lacrimal gland"], correct: 0, explanation: "Stapedius dysfunction (proximal/fallopian canal lesion) causes dysacusis; it localizes the lesion proximal to that branch." },
  { id: 8, question: "Which is a cause of BILATERAL facial palsy?", options: ["Sarcoidosis or Guillain-Barré syndrome", "Horner syndrome", "Refractive error", "Cataract"], correct: 0, explanation: "Bilateral CN VII palsy: Möbius, sarcoidosis, basilar meningitis, GBS, and NF2." },
  { id: 9, question: "First-line treatment of Bell palsy within 72 hours is:", options: ["Oral corticosteroids (7–10 days)", "Lifelong antivirals", "Surgery", "Botulinum toxin"], correct: 0, explanation: "Early oral corticosteroids improve outcomes; antiviral benefit is unclear." },
  { id: 10, question: "A poor prognostic factor in Bell palsy is:", options: ["Incomplete palsy", "Complete palsy at presentation with impaired lacrimation", "Young age", "Rapid partial recovery"], correct: 1, explanation: "Complete palsy, impaired lacrimation, dysacusis, pregnancy, diabetes, and age predict poorer recovery." },
  { id: 11, question: "A progressive (slowly worsening) facial palsy should raise concern for:", options: ["Bell palsy", "A neoplastic/infiltrative process", "Physiologic variation", "Benign myokymia"], correct: 1, explanation: "Progressive palsy suggests tumor invasion (brainstem, CPA, parotid) or infiltration — not idiopathic Bell palsy." },
  { id: 12, question: "The main danger to the eye in a facial palsy is:", options: ["Glaucoma", "Exposure keratopathy from poor lid closure", "Cataract", "Retinal detachment"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lagophthalmos causes corneal exposure; protect with lubricants, taping, moisture chambers, tarsorrhaphy, or lid weights." },
  { id: 13, question: "Emotional (involuntary) facial movement is preserved but voluntary lower-face movement is weak. This dissociation suggests a lesion that is:", options: ["Peripheral", "Supranuclear (voluntary pathway), sparing the extrapyramidal emotional pathway", "At the stylomastoid foramen", "In the parotid"], correct: 1, explanation: "Volitional-emotional dissociation indicates a supranuclear lesion affecting voluntary pathways while emotional (extrapyramidal) pathways are intact." },
];

// ── Eyelid · Ch 2 · Eyelid Position & Abnormal Facial Movements
const NEURO_LID2_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What should an eyelid examination assess?", answer: "General shape/appearance; blink rate (LOW in Parkinson disease, HIGH in blepharospasm); abnormal movements (e.g., oculofacial synkinesis); ptosis or contralateral lid retraction; facial motor strength (orbicularis oculi and other muscles) and lagophthalmos; reinnervation phenomena (synkinesis, crocodile tears from prior CN VII injury); and pupil/complete cranial-nerve function (to detect Horner or CN III palsy)." },
  { id: 2, question: "Distinguish true ptosis from pseudoptosis.", answer: "True ptosis is a low upper-lid margin from levator/Müller/neural/NMJ causes. Pseudoptosis mimics it: brow ptosis, lid laxity, and dermatochalasis; enophthalmos; hypotropia (the lid follows a downward-deviated eye); or contralateral eyelid retraction making the normal lid look ptotic." },
  { id: 3, question: "List the bedside signs of OCULAR myasthenia affecting the lids.", answer: "Fatigable (induced) ptosis on sustained upgaze, the peek sign (orbicularis fatigue on forced closure), enhanced ptosis / curtain sign (lifting one lid worsens the other), Cogan lid twitch, and the ice-pack and sleep/rest tests; confirmed with single-fiber EMG and repetitive nerve stimulation." },
  { id: 4, question: "What is apraxia of eyelid opening?", answer: "A rare, transient, NON-paralytic inability to initiate eyelid opening WITHOUT visible orbicularis contraction; the eyes open after touching the orbital rim or on sudden command. There is frontalis contraction (brow elevation) during attempts to open, and no other neural/myopathic signs; it can mimic ptosis." },
  { id: 5, question: "With what is apraxia of eyelid opening associated, and how is refractory disease treated?", answer: "Supranuclear extrapyramidal disease — Parkinson disease, multiple system atrophy, Huntington disease, Wilson disease, or progressive supranuclear palsy — and benign essential blepharospasm. Refractory cases: botulinum toxin type A, aponeurotic ptosis repair, orbicularis myectomy of the central eyelid, or frontalis suspension." },
  { id: 6, question: "Define eyelid retraction and give its causes.", answer: "Sclera visible ABOVE the superior corneal limbus in primary gaze. Causes: thyroid eye disease, dorsal midbrain syndrome (Collier sign), overcompensation for a contralateral ptosis, and in infants the physiologic 'setting-sun' sign (preterm) and 'eye-popping reflex' (bilateral retraction when ambient light is reduced)." },
  { id: 7, question: "Describe benign essential blepharospasm (BEB).", answer: "Bilateral episodic involuntary contraction of the orbicularis oculi (eyelids may be forced shut), abating during sleep, onset age 40–60. With facial grimacing/other movements it is Meige syndrome. Exclude REFLEX blepharospasm (severe dry eye, intraocular inflammation, meningeal irritation — usually with photophobia). First-line treatment: botulinum toxin type A into the orbicularis; also FL-41 tinted lenses and surgical myectomy." },
  { id: 8, question: "Describe hemifacial spasm — features and cause.", answer: "Unilateral episodic spasms of facial musculature (seconds to minutes) that CAN occur during sleep; CN VII function is usually intact but subtle ipsilateral facial weakness may develop. Cause: compression of the CN VII root exit zone by a dolichoectatic vessel (also prior Bell palsy, CN VII injury, or demyelination)." },
  { id: 9, question: "How is hemifacial spasm evaluated and treated?", answer: "MRI of the brainstem with MRA (to find a compressing vessel). First-line: botulinum toxin injection (lower dose than BEB, reinjection every 3–4 months). Medications: carbamazepine, clonazepam, or baclofen. Definitive surgery: microvascular decompression (suboccipital craniectomy with a sponge between CN VII and the vessel)." },
  { id: 10, question: "Contrast spastic-paretic hemifacial contracture and facial myokymia.", answer: "Spastic-paretic hemifacial contracture: rare unilateral facial contracture WITH weakness — myokymia of the orbicularis spreads to the ipsilateral face with tonic contracture over weeks–months, from pontine CN VII nucleus dysfunction (e.g., pontine neoplasm). Facial myokymia: continuous unilateral fine undulating/fibrillary contraction (orbicularis then facial muscles) from intramedullary pontine disease — pontine glioma, MS in adults, or GBS in children." },
  { id: 11, question: "What is benign eyelid myokymia and how is it managed?", answer: "A common, intermittent fluttering of the orbicularis oculi, usually benign and self-limited. If it lasts longer than a month it may benefit from botulinum toxin; otherwise, stop contributing factors — caffeine, stress, and sleep deprivation." },
  { id: 12, question: "List other conditions that can mimic abnormal eyelid/facial movements.", answer: "Focal cortical seizures, Todd paralysis, tardive dyskinesia (neuroleptics/antipsychotics), oral-facial dyskinesias (major tranquilizers), habit spasm/tic (promptly inhibited on command), and Tourette syndrome (may present with facial twitching)." },
  { id: 13, question: "How does blink rate help localize eyelid/facial disorders?", answer: "A LOW blink rate suggests Parkinson disease (reduced dopaminergic drive), whereas a HIGH blink rate or forced involuntary closures suggests blepharospasm/dystonia — a quick clue toward an extrapyramidal cause." },
];

const NEURO_LID2_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Lid margin appears low because the eye is deviated downward. This is:", options: ["True aponeurotic ptosis", "Pseudoptosis from hypotropia", "Horner ptosis", "Myasthenic ptosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hypotropia causes pseudoptosis — the lid follows the eye; correcting fixation eliminates the 'ptosis.'" },
  { id: 2, question: "A LOW blink rate is characteristic of:", options: ["Benign essential blepharospasm", "Parkinson disease", "Hemifacial spasm", "Myasthenia"], correct: 1, explanation: "Parkinson disease reduces the blink rate; blepharospasm increases blinking/closure." },
  { id: 3, question: "Inability to initiate lid opening without orbicularis contraction, relieved by touching the orbital rim, is:", options: ["Myasthenic ptosis", "Apraxia of eyelid opening", "Horner syndrome", "Dermatochalasis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Apraxia of eyelid opening is a non-paralytic failure to initiate opening, with frontalis overaction and no orbicularis spasm visible." },
  { id: 4, question: "Apraxia of eyelid opening is associated with:", options: ["Extrapyramidal disease (e.g., PSP, Parkinson) and blepharospasm", "Cataract", "Keratoconus", "Optic neuritis"], correct: 0, explanation: "It accompanies extrapyramidal disorders (PSP, PD, MSA, Huntington, Wilson) and benign essential blepharospasm." },
  { id: 5, question: "Eyelid retraction (scleral show above the limbus) is caused by all EXCEPT:", options: ["Thyroid eye disease", "Dorsal midbrain syndrome (Collier sign)", "Horner syndrome", "Overcompensation for contralateral ptosis"], correct: 2, explanation: "Horner causes mild PTOSIS, not retraction; TED, Collier sign, and contralateral ptosis overcompensation cause retraction." },
  { id: 6, question: "First-line treatment of benign essential blepharospasm is:", options: ["Oral steroids", "Botulinum toxin type A into the orbicularis oculi", "Microvascular decompression", "Carbamazepine"], correct: 1, explanation: "BoNT-A into the orbicularis is first-line for BEB; FL-41 tint and myectomy are adjuncts." },
  { id: 7, question: "Benign essential blepharospasm characteristically:", options: ["Is unilateral and persists in sleep", "Is bilateral and abates during sleep", "Involves the limbs first", "Causes facial weakness"], correct: 1, explanation: "BEB is bilateral, episodic orbicularis spasm that stops during sleep; onset age 40–60 (Meige if facial grimacing)." },
  { id: 8, question: "Hemifacial spasm is most often caused by:", options: ["Vascular compression of the CN VII root exit zone", "A cortical seizure", "Thyroid disease", "A refractive error"], correct: 0, explanation: "A dolichoectatic vessel compressing the facial nerve root exit zone is the classic cause; MRI/MRA is indicated." },
  { id: 9, question: "A feature distinguishing hemifacial spasm from blepharospasm is that hemifacial spasm:", options: ["Is bilateral and stops in sleep", "Is unilateral and can persist during sleep", "Spares the lower face", "Never involves CN VII"], correct: 1, explanation: "Hemifacial spasm is unilateral and may continue in sleep; BEB is bilateral and abates in sleep." },
  { id: 10, question: "The definitive surgical treatment for hemifacial spasm from vascular compression is:", options: ["Myectomy", "Microvascular decompression", "Tarsorrhaphy", "Frontalis suspension"], correct: 1, explanation: "Microvascular decompression places a cushion between CN VII and the offending vessel; BoNT is first-line symptomatic therapy." },
  { id: 11, question: "Continuous fine undulating facial muscle contraction (facial myokymia) in an adult suggests:", options: ["Multiple sclerosis or a pontine lesion", "Bell palsy", "Cataract", "Dry eye"], correct: 0, explanation: "Facial myokymia reflects intramedullary pontine disease — MS in adults, pontine glioma, or GBS in children." },
  { id: 12, question: "Benign eyelid myokymia is best managed initially by:", options: ["Botulinum toxin immediately", "Stopping caffeine, stress, and sleep deprivation", "Microvascular decompression", "Oral steroids"], correct: 1, explanation: "It is self-limited; address caffeine/stress/sleep; BoNT only if it persists beyond a month." },
  { id: 13, question: "A facial twitch that is promptly inhibited on command is most consistent with:", options: ["Hemifacial spasm", "A habit spasm/tic", "Facial myokymia", "Blepharospasm"], correct: 1, explanation: "Habit spasms/tics (and Tourette) can be voluntarily suppressed briefly, unlike organic spasm/myokymia." },
];

