// ── Meds · Ch 1 · Cholinergic Agents (Muscarinic & Nicotinic)
const NEURO_MED1_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Which ocular structures carry cholinergic (muscarinic) vs adrenergic innervation?", answer: "Muscarinic (parasympathetic): iris sphincter and ciliary muscle. Adrenergic (sympathetic): iris dilator and Müller (superior palpebral) muscle. Both systems act on the ciliary epithelium/processes, trabecular meshwork, and ocular blood vessel smooth muscle." },
  { id: 2, question: "What are the three main actions of a topical direct-acting muscarinic agonist?", answer: "(1) Iris sphincter contraction → miosis (alters iris–lens/angle relationship); (2) ciliary muscle CIRCULAR fiber contraction → accommodation (lens moves forward, becomes more spherical); (3) ciliary muscle LONGITUDINAL fiber contraction → pulls the scleral spur, opens the trabecular meshwork → ↑ aqueous outflow → ↓ IOP." },
  { id: 3, question: "Compare acetylcholine, carbachol, and pilocarpine as direct muscarinic agonists.", answer: "Acetylcholine: no useful topical form (poor corneal penetration, degraded by AChE); 1% intracameral acts in seconds but is very short-lived. Carbachol 0.01% intracameral: ~100× more potent/longer than intracameral ACh (max miosis ~5 min, ~24-hour duration), also lowers IOP in the first 24 h post-op. Pilocarpine (topical): 0.12% to confirm an Adie tonic pupil, 1.25% FDA-approved once-daily for presbyopia; >2% is no more effective and can cause a paradoxical IOP rise (anterior lens–iris movement) in angle-closure." },
  { id: 4, question: "List the ocular adverse effects of miotics.", answer: "Miosis (poor scotopic/night vision, worse with axial cataract, fall risk in older adults), cataractogenesis, induced myopia/accommodative spasm (more in patients <50), and — at higher concentrations — iris cysts and retinal detachment (ciliary traction on the peripheral retina)." },
  { id: 5, question: "How do indirect-acting muscarinic agonists (cholinesterase inhibitors) work and what are the classes?", answer: "They bind the serine hydroxyl site of cholinesterase, forming an enzyme–inhibitor complex so acetylcholine is not hydrolyzed → more ACh at synapses (same effects as direct agonists but more potent/longer). Reversible: physostigmine, neostigmine, edrophonium. Irreversible (organophosphates): echothiophate, DFP, demecarium — very stable covalent bonds; oximes (pralidoxime) reverse acute organophosphate poisoning." },
  { id: 6, question: "What are the key anesthesia/surgical implications of long-term echothiophate?", answer: "It reduces plasma (butyryl)cholinesterase, so ester local anesthetics (e.g., procaine) that rely on plasma cholinesterase have increased toxicity, and succinylcholine is not metabolized normally → prolonged respiratory paralysis under general anesthesia. In children it can cause iris pigment epithelial cysts at the pupil margin (minimized with phenylephrine 2.5%)." },
  { id: 7, question: "What are the actions and uses of topical muscarinic ANTAGONISTS (cycloplegics/mydriatics)?", answer: "They block postsynaptic muscarinic receptors → iris sphincter paralysis with unopposed dilator → mydriasis, and ciliary muscle paralysis → cycloplegia. Uses: diagnostic dilation to examine the periphery; anterior uveitis (break/prevent posterior synechiae, reduce pain, reduce blood–aqueous permeability); accurate cycloplegic refraction in children; and ciliary-block (malignant) glaucoma." },
  { id: 8, question: "Rank cycloplegics by potency/duration and give a caution.", answer: "Potent/long: atropine (a single drop can last days; 2–3×/day for full cycloplegia), scopolamine, homatropine. Shorter-acting: cyclopentolate, tropicamide (preferred when dilation is only for exam/refraction). Caution in POAG and narrow angles — cycloplegic dilation can raise IOP; prefer short-acting agents." },
  { id: 9, question: "Describe systemic anticholinergic toxicity from topical cycloplegics and its treatment.", answer: "Dose-related central and peripheral anticholinergic effects (especially in children): flushing, fever, tachycardia, constipation, urinary retention, and delirium ('hot, dry, red, mad'). Mild: stop the drug. Severe: IV physostigmine (a tertiary amine that crosses the blood–brain barrier), titrated slowly." },
  { id: 10, question: "What is the role of edrophonium and neostigmine as NICOTINIC-relevant agents?", answer: "Edrophonium is a short-acting, competitive (non-covalent) cholinesterase inhibitor used to diagnose myasthenia gravis — AChE inhibition raises synaptic ACh and improves transmission despite fewer receptors; give IV atropine 0.4–0.6 mg with it to blunt muscarinic effects (vomiting, diarrhea, bradycardia). Neostigmine is a longer-acting IM agent also used for MG diagnosis, allowing assessment of more complex endpoints (e.g., orthoptic measurements)." },
  { id: 11, question: "Contrast depolarizing and nondepolarizing nicotinic antagonists, and the ocular hazard.", answer: "Nondepolarizing (curare-like: rocuronium, vecuronium, pancuronium) are competitive antagonists → flaccid paralysis. Depolarizing (succinylcholine, decamethonium) first depolarize (contraction) then cause flaccidity in singly-innervated fibers; but in the multiply-innervated ('en grappe') ~1/5 of extraocular muscle fibers they cause SUSTAINED contraction that raises IOP — so succinylcholine is avoided in open-globe injuries." },
  { id: 12, question: "Why can systemic/surgical atropine affect the oculocardiac reflex?", answer: "Systemic atropine (an antimuscarinic) blocks the efferent vagal limb of the oculocardiac reflex, reducing reflex bradycardia during eye surgery/manipulation; the reflex can also be blunted at the afferent limb by retrobulbar anesthesia." },
];

const NEURO_MED1_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Pilocarpine lowers IOP mainly by:", options: ["Reducing aqueous production", "Ciliary longitudinal muscle contraction pulling the scleral spur to open the trabecular meshwork", "Blocking the dilator", "Constricting episcleral veins"], correct: 1, explanation: "Direct muscarinic agonists increase trabecular outflow via ciliary longitudinal contraction on the scleral spur." },
  { id: 2, question: "Which concentration of pilocarpine confirms an Adie (tonic) pupil?", options: ["0.12% (dilute)", "4%", "10%", "2%"], correct: 0, explanation: "A denervation-supersensitive tonic pupil constricts to dilute pilocarpine (~0.1–0.12%), which barely affects a normal pupil." },
  { id: 3, question: "A major visual disability from miotics is:", options: ["Mydriasis", "Poor night (scotopic) vision", "Hyperopia", "Diplopia"], correct: 1, explanation: "Miosis impairs dim-light vision and worsens symptoms with axial cataract, raising fall risk in older adults." },
  { id: 4, question: "Echothiophate (an irreversible cholinesterase inhibitor) is dangerous with general anesthesia because it:", options: ["Raises plasma cholinesterase", "Lowers plasma cholinesterase, prolonging succinylcholine paralysis", "Blocks nicotinic receptors", "Causes mydriasis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Reduced plasma (butyryl)cholinesterase prolongs succinylcholine and ester-anesthetic effects." },
  { id: 5, question: "Oximes such as pralidoxime are used to:", options: ["Enhance miosis", "Reverse acute organophosphate poisoning", "Treat glaucoma long-term", "Dilate the pupil"], correct: 1, explanation: "Pralidoxime reactivates phosphorylated cholinesterase in acute organophosphate toxicity." },
  { id: 6, question: "Topical muscarinic ANTAGONISTS cause mydriasis by:", options: ["Stimulating the dilator", "Paralyzing the iris sphincter (unopposed dilator)", "Blocking α1 receptors", "Contracting the ciliary muscle"], correct: 1, explanation: "Antimuscarinics paralyze the sphincter and ciliary muscle → mydriasis and cycloplegia." },
  { id: 7, question: "The longest-acting cycloplegic, lasting days from a single drop, is:", options: ["Tropicamide", "Cyclopentolate", "Atropine", "Phenylephrine"], correct: 2, explanation: "Atropine produces the most prolonged cycloplegia/mydriasis; tropicamide is short-acting." },
  { id: 8, question: "Systemic anticholinergic toxicity in a child after cycloplegic drops is treated (if severe) with:", options: ["IV physostigmine", "IV atropine", "Oral pilocarpine", "Naloxone"], correct: 0, explanation: "Physostigmine (crosses the BBB) reverses central/peripheral antimuscarinic toxicity (flushing, fever, delirium)." },
  { id: 9, question: "Edrophonium is used to:", options: ["Treat glaucoma", "Diagnose myasthenia gravis", "Reverse mydriasis", "Lower ICP"], correct: 1, explanation: "Short-acting AChE inhibition transiently improves myasthenic weakness; co-give atropine to limit muscarinic effects." },
  { id: 10, question: "Succinylcholine is avoided in open-globe injury because it can:", options: ["Dilate the pupil", "Cause sustained extraocular muscle contraction and raise IOP", "Lower blood pressure", "Block the oculocardiac reflex"], correct: 1, explanation: "Depolarizing blockers cause tonic contraction in multiply-innervated EOM fibers, raising IOP — hazardous with an open globe." },
  { id: 11, question: "Carbachol 0.01% intracameral is used in cataract surgery mainly to:", options: ["Dilate the pupil", "Produce miosis and lower IOP in the first 24 hours", "Paralyze accommodation", "Reverse mydriasis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Intracameral carbachol gives durable miosis and controls the early postoperative IOP spike." },
  { id: 12, question: "Induced myopia/accommodative spasm from miotics is most common in:", options: ["Patients over 70", "Patients under 50", "Infants only", "Aphakic eyes"], correct: 1, explanation: "Younger patients (<50) with active accommodation develop ciliary-contraction myopia from miotics." },
  { id: 13, question: "Systemic atropine during eye surgery can:", options: ["Trigger the oculocardiac reflex", "Block the oculocardiac reflex (reduce reflex bradycardia)", "Cause miosis", "Raise plasma cholinesterase"], correct: 1, explanation: "Antimuscarinic atropine blocks the vagal efferent limb, reducing oculocardiac-reflex bradycardia." },
  { id: 14, question: "At >2%, pilocarpine in an angle-closure–prone eye can cause:", options: ["Lower IOP always", "A paradoxical IOP rise from anterior lens–iris movement", "Mydriasis", "Cycloplegia"], correct: 1, explanation: "High-concentration pilocarpine can rotate the ciliary body/lens-iris forward, paradoxically raising IOP in susceptible eyes." },
];

// ── Meds · Ch 2 · Adrenergic Agents
const NEURO_MED2_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is topical phenylephrine's action and its main systemic risk?", answer: "A direct α1-adrenergic agonist that stimulates the iris dilator → mydriasis. Systemic absorption raises blood pressure — important in infants (large dose relative to body weight), patients sensitive to α-agonists, and those on reserpine, tricyclic antidepressants, cocaine, or MAO inhibitors." },
  { id: 2, question: "Why may phenylephrine alone give inadequate dilation, and what are 10% risks?", answer: "The parasympathetic sphincter is stronger than the dilator and the light reflex overcomes dilation during ophthalmoscopy — so it is combined with a cycloplegic for sustained mydriasis. 10% phenylephrine can cause stroke, MI, cardiac arrest, and baroreceptor-mediated bradycardia (especially in vasculopaths on systemic β-blockers)." },
  { id: 3, question: "What is Omidria and its purpose?", answer: "A surgical irrigating-solution additive of phenylephrine 1% + ketorolac 0.3%, FDA-approved to prevent intraoperative miosis and reduce postoperative pain in cataract surgery; it also helps manage intraoperative floppy iris syndrome (IFIS) in patients on tamsulosin." },
  { id: 4, question: "Describe apraclonidine — mechanism, IOP effects, and a key diagnostic use.", answer: "An α2-adrenergic agonist (clonidine derivative): presynaptic α2 stimulation reduces norepinephrine release and (via adenylate cyclase inhibition) decreases aqueous production, lowers episcleral venous pressure, and increases trabecular outflow — useful for peri-laser/surgery IOP spikes. Its weak α1 activity reverses the anisocoria of Horner syndrome (denervation-supersensitive α1 receptors dilate the Horner pupil), a key diagnostic use. Tachyphylaxis limits long-term use." },
  { id: 5, question: "Compare brimonidine with apraclonidine.", answer: "Brimonidine is more α2-selective, more lipophilic, and has less tachyphylaxis; it causes less allergy (follicular conjunctivitis/contact blepharodermatitis <15% vs up to 40% for apraclonidine) with minimal cross-sensitivity, and does not cause posterior-segment/optic-nerve vasoconstriction (apraclonidine has higher α1 affinity → more vasoconstriction)." },
  { id: 6, question: "What is oxymetazoline 0.1% approved for in neuro-ophthalmology?", answer: "A mixed α1 + α2 agonist, oxymetazoline 0.1% once daily is approved for acquired (aponeurotic) ptosis in adults — it stimulates Müller (superior tarsal) muscle to raise the lid a few millimeters." },
  { id: 7, question: "What are the safety cautions for α2 agonists?", answer: "Use caution in severe cardiovascular disease, with MAO inhibitors or tricyclics, and with β-blockers/antihypertensives/cardiac glycosides. Brimonidine crosses the blood–brain barrier (fatigue, drowsiness). They are CONTRAINDICATED in infants (reported severe systemic toxicity — hypotension, hypothermia, bradycardia, apnea) and used cautiously in young children." },
  { id: 8, question: "Which indirect-acting adrenergic agents test sympathetic innervation, and what is reuptake's role?", answer: "Cocaine (4% or 10%) and hydroxyamphetamine 1% (compounded). Released norepinephrine at the terminal is mainly inactivated by reuptake into presynaptic granules (~70% recaptured) — cocaine blocks this reuptake, and hydroxyamphetamine releases stored NE, which is the basis of Horner localization testing." },
  { id: 9, question: "Describe the ophthalmic α1-antagonists thymoxamine and dapiprazole.", answer: "Thymoxamine (moxisylyte; Europe, not US): competitive α1 blockade → blocks the dilator → miosis with minimal ciliary effect (no major change in anterior chamber depth, outflow, IOP, or accommodation in POAG); helps differentiate angle-closure glaucoma from POAG with narrow angles and reverses phenylephrine mydriasis. Dapiprazole (no longer in US): reverses phenylephrine- and tropicamide-induced mydriasis in ~30 minutes by acting on the dilator (not the ciliary muscle), but does NOT reverse cycloplegia." },
  { id: 10, question: "What is first-line topical therapy for glaucoma among adrenergic agents, and where are adrenergic receptors in/around the eye?", answer: "β-adrenergic antagonists (β-blockers) are first-line topical glaucoma therapy. Adrenergic receptors are on the iris dilator, Müller (superior palpebral) muscle, ciliary epithelium/processes, trabecular meshwork, and ocular blood-vessel smooth muscle — innervated by postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion." },
  { id: 11, question: "Why is phenylephrine typically combined with a cycloplegic for dilation?", answer: "Phenylephrine only stimulates the dilator; the stronger parasympathetic sphincter and the pupillary light reflex can overcome it, so adding an antimuscarinic (which paralyzes the sphincter) gives fuller, more sustained mydriasis for examination." },
];

const NEURO_MED2_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Topical phenylephrine dilates the pupil by stimulating the:", options: ["Iris sphincter", "Iris dilator (α1)", "Ciliary muscle", "Müller muscle only"], correct: 1, explanation: "Phenylephrine is a direct α1 agonist acting on the iris dilator → mydriasis." },
  { id: 2, question: "10% phenylephrine is risky mainly because of:", options: ["Hypoglycemia", "Systemic hypertension (stroke, MI, reflex bradycardia)", "Hypothermia", "Miosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "High-concentration phenylephrine can cause dangerous BP spikes and baroreceptor bradycardia, especially in vasculopaths." },
  { id: 3, question: "Apraclonidine reverses the anisocoria of Horner syndrome because the Horner pupil has:", options: ["Normal α1 receptors", "Denervation-supersensitive α1 receptors that respond to its weak α1 activity", "Paralyzed sphincter", "No sympathetic supply ever"], correct: 1, explanation: "Upregulated/supersensitive α1 receptors make the Horner pupil dilate to apraclonidine's weak α1 effect, reversing the anisocoria." },
  { id: 4, question: "Compared with apraclonidine, brimonidine:", options: ["Has more tachyphylaxis", "Is more α2-selective with less allergy and no posterior vasoconstriction", "Causes more conjunctivitis", "Has higher α1 affinity"], correct: 1, explanation: "Brimonidine is more α2-selective, lipophilic, better tolerated, and lacks posterior-segment vasoconstriction." },
  { id: 5, question: "α2 agonists (apraclonidine/brimonidine) are CONTRAINDICATED in:", options: ["Adults with glaucoma", "Infants (severe systemic toxicity)", "Patients with dry eye", "Myopes"], correct: 1, explanation: "Infants can develop hypotension, hypothermia, bradycardia, and apnea; α2 agonists are contraindicated in them." },
  { id: 6, question: "Oxymetazoline 0.1% once daily is approved for:", options: ["Glaucoma", "Acquired ptosis (via Müller muscle)", "Dry eye", "Allergic conjunctivitis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Oxymetazoline (mixed α1/α2) stimulates Müller muscle to elevate the lid in acquired aponeurotic ptosis." },
  { id: 7, question: "Cocaine's mechanism in Horner testing is to:", options: ["Release stored norepinephrine", "Block norepinephrine reuptake (so a Horner pupil fails to dilate)", "Block α1 receptors", "Stimulate the sphincter"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cocaine blocks NE reuptake; with no NE being released (Horner), the pupil fails to dilate." },
  { id: 8, question: "Thymoxamine (an α1 antagonist) causes:", options: ["Mydriasis", "Miosis (blocks the dilator) with minimal ciliary effect", "Cycloplegia", "Raised IOP"], correct: 1, explanation: "α1 blockade blocks the dilator → miosis; it can reverse phenylephrine mydriasis and help distinguish angle-closure from narrow-angle POAG." },
  { id: 9, question: "First-line TOPICAL adrenergic therapy for glaucoma is:", options: ["α1 agonists", "β-adrenergic antagonists (β-blockers)", "α1 antagonists", "Indirect agonists"], correct: 1, explanation: "Topical β-blockers are a first-line class for lowering IOP in glaucoma." },
  { id: 10, question: "Why is phenylephrine often combined with a cycloplegic?", options: ["To lower IOP", "Because the sphincter/light reflex can overcome dilator stimulation alone", "To reverse mydriasis", "To treat uveitis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Adding an antimuscarinic paralyzes the sphincter, giving fuller, more sustained dilation." },
  { id: 11, question: "Omidria (phenylephrine + ketorolac) is used intraoperatively to:", options: ["Induce miosis", "Prevent intraoperative miosis and reduce pain (and help IFIS)", "Dilate before refraction", "Reverse mydriasis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Added to irrigation, it maintains dilation during cataract surgery, reduces pain, and aids IFIS management in tamsulosin users." },
  { id: 12, question: "Dapiprazole reverses pharmacologic mydriasis caused by:", options: ["Atropine (cycloplegics)", "Phenylephrine and tropicamide (acting on the dilator)", "Pilocarpine", "Brimonidine"], correct: 1, explanation: "Dapiprazole (α1 antagonist) reverses phenylephrine/tropicamide mydriasis via the dilator but not cycloplegia." },
  { id: 13, question: "Adrenergic receptors around the eye are supplied by postganglionic fibers from the:", options: ["Ciliary ganglion", "Superior cervical ganglion", "Pterygopalatine ganglion", "Geniculate ganglion"], correct: 1, explanation: "Sympathetic (adrenergic) postganglionic fibers arise from the superior cervical ganglion." },
];

// ── Meds · Ch 3 · Diagnostic Pharmacology: MG, Anisocoria & Horner
const NEURO_MED3_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Why are the PUPILS normal in myasthenia gravis?", answer: "MG reduces the availability of NICOTINIC acetylcholine receptors on skeletal muscle; the iris sphincter/pupil are controlled by MUSCARINIC receptors, which are unaffected — so the pupil is always normal in MG." },
  { id: 2, question: "List drug classes that can AGGRAVATE myasthenia gravis.", answer: "Antiarrhythmics, statins, certain antibiotics (fluoroquinolones, macrolides, aminoglycosides), chemotherapeutic agents, antiepileptics, penicillamine, corticosteroids (transient early worsening), β-blockers, and calcium channel blockers." },
  { id: 3, question: "Describe the edrophonium and neostigmine tests for MG.", answer: "Edrophonium chloride: a short-acting IV cholinesterase inhibitor that transiently improves weakness. Neostigmine methylsulfate: a longer-acting IM agent (given with atropine) useful in children/adults without ptosis when prolonged observation is needed for ocular alignment — a positive test is clinical improvement within 30–45 minutes. Adverse effects: salivation, fasciculations, GI discomfort." },
  { id: 4, question: "Define anisocoria and physiologic anisocoria.", answer: "Unequal pupil diameters from asymmetric efferent input to the iris muscles. Physiologic (simple) anisocoria occurs in ~20% of people, is usually <1.0 mm, and varies from day to day with a normal light reaction and equal dynamics in light and dark." },
  { id: 5, question: "When does denervation supersensitivity develop after a sympathetic lesion, and why does it matter?", answer: "Supersensitivity of the iris dilator α1 receptors develops several (≈2–5) days after denervation; it underlies the apraclonidine test for Horner syndrome (the supersensitive Horner pupil dilates), so very acute Horner syndrome may test falsely negative before supersensitivity sets in." },
  { id: 6, question: "Outline pharmacologic testing of Horner syndrome (confirmation step).", answer: "For anisocoria greater (or equal) in dim light: apraclonidine 0.5–1% (read ~60 min) — REVERSAL of anisocoria confirms Horner (no response = physiologic); OR cocaine 4–10% (read ~45 min) — residual anisocoria ≥1 mm confirms Horner (≤1 mm = physiologic, because a normal pupil dilates to cocaine while a Horner pupil does not)." },
  { id: 7, question: "How does hydroxyamphetamine LOCALIZE a Horner lesion?", answer: "Hydroxyamphetamine 1% releases stored norepinephrine from an intact postganglionic (third-order) terminal. If BOTH pupils dilate, the lesion is first- or second-order (postganglionic neuron intact); if the small (Horner) pupil fails to dilate (remains unchanged), the lesion is THIRD-order (postganglionic). It is done 24–72 hours apart from cocaine testing." },
  { id: 8, question: "How is dilute pilocarpine used to confirm a tonic (Adie) pupil or CN III palsy?", answer: "The denervated iris sphincter is supersensitive to weak parasympathomimetics: dilute pilocarpine (0.1%) constricts an Adie pupil in ~80% (over 5–7 days of denervation) and a CN III-palsy pupil in up to two-thirds, whereas a normal pupil shows little response." },
  { id: 9, question: "List the clinical features of Horner syndrome by denervated structure.", answer: "Müller muscle → ptosis; iris dilator → miosis and dilation lag; sweat glands → anhidrosis; iris pigment → heterochromia (lighter iris); inferior tarsal muscle → 'upside-down ptosis' and apparent enophthalmos; conjunctival vessels → conjunctival injection." },
  { id: 10, question: "Summarize the medical treatment options for myasthenia gravis.", answer: "Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors (neostigmine, pyridostigmine) for symptoms; corticosteroids; immunosuppressants (azathioprine, cyclosporine, mycophenolate, tacrolimus, methotrexate, cyclophosphamide) and biologics (eculizumab, ravulizumab, rozanolixizumab, zilucoplan, efgartigimod, tocilizumab, rituximab); and IVIG or plasmapheresis for severe/crisis disease." },
  { id: 11, question: "Why is the cocaine test positive (no dilation) regardless of the Horner lesion's order?", answer: "Cocaine blocks reuptake of norepinephrine at the junction, so a normal pupil dilates; ANY Horner lesion (first-, second-, or third-order) reduces NE release, so the Horner pupil fails to dilate — the test confirms Horner but cannot localize it (hydroxyamphetamine localizes)." },
];

const NEURO_MED3_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "The pupil is normal in myasthenia gravis because MG affects:", options: ["Muscarinic receptors", "Nicotinic receptors (skeletal muscle), not the muscarinic pupil", "The optic nerve", "The ciliary ganglion"], correct: 1, explanation: "MG targets nicotinic ACh receptors; the muscarinic pupillary sphincter is spared." },
  { id: 2, question: "Which drug can aggravate myasthenia gravis?", options: ["Acetaminophen", "Fluoroquinolone/macrolide antibiotics", "Artificial tears", "Vitamin C"], correct: 1, explanation: "Certain antibiotics (quinolones, macrolides, aminoglycosides), β-blockers, statins, penicillamine, and others can worsen MG." },
  { id: 3, question: "A positive neostigmine test shows clinical improvement within:", options: ["Seconds", "30–45 minutes", "6 hours", "2 days"], correct: 1, explanation: "Intramuscular neostigmine (with atropine) improves signs within ~30–45 minutes if MG is present." },
  { id: 4, question: "Physiologic anisocoria is typically:", options: [">2 mm and fixed", "<1 mm and variable day to day with normal reactions", "Associated with ptosis", "Only in dim light"], correct: 1, explanation: "Simple anisocoria affects ~20% of people, is usually <1 mm, variable, with normal light reactions." },
  { id: 5, question: "Apraclonidine confirms Horner syndrome when the affected pupil:", options: ["Fails to dilate", "Dilates, reversing the anisocoria", "Constricts strongly", "Shows no change bilaterally"], correct: 1, explanation: "Denervation-supersensitive α1 receptors make the Horner pupil dilate to apraclonidine's weak α1 effect." },
  { id: 6, question: "On cocaine testing, a residual anisocoria of ≥1 mm indicates:", options: ["Physiologic anisocoria", "Horner syndrome", "Adie pupil", "CN III palsy"], correct: 1, explanation: "A Horner pupil fails to dilate to cocaine (no NE reuptake to block), leaving ≥1 mm anisocoria." },
  { id: 7, question: "Hydroxyamphetamine fails to dilate the Horner pupil. This localizes the lesion to the:", options: ["First-order neuron", "Second-order neuron", "Third-order (postganglionic) neuron", "Optic nerve"], correct: 2, explanation: "Hydroxyamphetamine releases stored NE from an intact postganglionic terminal; failure to dilate means that neuron (third-order) is damaged." },
  { id: 8, question: "Dilute pilocarpine 0.1% constricting a dilated pupil supports:", options: ["Pharmacologic (atropinic) mydriasis", "A tonic (Adie) pupil from denervation supersensitivity", "Horner syndrome", "Physiologic anisocoria"], correct: 1, explanation: "Cholinergic denervation supersensitivity makes an Adie (or CN III) pupil constrict to very weak pilocarpine; a normal pupil does not." },
  { id: 9, question: "The cocaine test confirms Horner syndrome but:", options: ["Also localizes the lesion", "Cannot localize it (hydroxyamphetamine localizes)", "Only works in third-order lesions", "Requires dilation first"], correct: 1, explanation: "Any Horner order fails cocaine dilation; hydroxyamphetamine is needed to separate pre- from postganglionic." },
  { id: 10, question: "Very acute Horner syndrome may test falsely negative with apraclonidine because:", options: ["The pupil is too big", "Denervation supersensitivity takes ~2–5 days to develop", "Cocaine was used first", "The eye is inflamed"], correct: 1, explanation: "α1 supersensitivity needs several days to develop; apraclonidine may not yet reverse a hyperacute Horner." },
  { id: 11, question: "Rapid short-term improvement in a myasthenic crisis is achieved with:", options: ["Pyridostigmine tablets", "IVIG or plasmapheresis", "Botulinum toxin", "Topical steroids"], correct: 1, explanation: "IVIG or plasma exchange gives rapid improvement; AChE inhibitors and chronic immunosuppression are for maintenance." },
  { id: 12, question: "'Upside-down ptosis' (slight lower-lid elevation) in Horner reflects denervation of the:", options: ["Levator", "Inferior tarsal muscle", "Orbicularis", "Ciliary muscle"], correct: 1, explanation: "Loss of inferior tarsal muscle tone raises the lower lid, contributing to apparent enophthalmos in Horner syndrome." },
  { id: 13, question: "Edrophonium's muscarinic side effects (bradycardia, vomiting) are blunted by co-administering:", options: ["Atropine", "Pilocarpine", "Phenylephrine", "Timolol"], correct: 0, explanation: "IV atropine 0.4–0.6 mg counters the muscarinic effects of the AChE inhibitor during the edrophonium test." },
];

// ── Meds · Ch 4 · Anti-inflammatory, Immunomodulators & IIH Drugs
const NEURO_MED4_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Summarize the anti-inflammatory mechanisms of corticosteroids.", answer: "Genomic: bind a cytosolic receptor → the complex alters gene transcription. Tissue: reduce early inflammation (heat, congestion, edema, pain) and late inflammation (capillary/fibroblast proliferation, collagen, scarring). Biochemical: inhibit arachidonic acid release → ↓ prostaglandins/leukotrienes/thromboxanes and ↓ lysosomal enzyme release. Immune: act mainly on the EFFERENT limb (↓ macrophage recruitment; systemic use sequesters T lymphocytes) without changing IgE/IgG titers." },
  { id: 2, question: "List the OCULAR adverse effects of corticosteroids.", answer: "Glaucoma (steroid response), posterior subcapsular cataract, worsening of bacterial/viral (especially herpetic) and fungal infection, ptosis, mydriasis, scleral melting, eyelid skin atrophy, pseudohypopyon (after intraocular injection), and central serous chorioretinopathy." },
  { id: 3, question: "List the major SYSTEMIC adverse effects of oral corticosteroids.", answer: "HPA-axis suppression, hyperglycemia/diabetes, muscle wasting, osteoporosis, truncal fat redistribution, CNS effects (euphoria, insomnia, psychosis), aseptic (avascular) necrosis of the hip, and peptic ulcer." },
  { id: 4, question: "Name neuro-ophthalmic conditions in which corticosteroids are used.", answer: "Optic neuritis (MS, NMOSD, MOGAD, post-viral), optic perineuritis (sarcoidosis, GPA, IgG4-related disease, GCA, MOGAD), arteritic AION (GCA), nonspecific orbital inflammation (NSOI), dysthyroid optic neuropathy, myasthenia gravis, possibly traumatic optic neuropathy, idiopathic cavernous-sinus inflammation (Tolosa-Hunt syndrome), Bell palsy, and radiation-induced optic neuropathy." },
  { id: 5, question: "Which immunosuppressants/biologics are used for NMOSD, MOGAD, and GCA?", answer: "NMOSD: rituximab, eculizumab, ravulizumab, inebilizumab, satralizumab. MOGAD: rituximab, mycophenolate mofetil, IVIG, tocilizumab. Arteritic AION (GCA): tocilizumab (plus corticosteroids)." },
  { id: 6, question: "Give examples of MS disease-modifying therapies and their ocular side effects.", answer: "Interferons, glatiramer acetate, fumarates, fingolimod, natalizumab, alemtuzumab, teriflunomide, cladribine, ocrelizumab, ofatumumab, ublituximab. Ocular notes: fingolimod → macular edema (FAME, ~0.4–1% within 3 months; higher risk with diabetes, uveitis, recent cataract surgery); alemtuzumab → Graves disease/thyroid eye disease; teriflunomide → optic neuritis, macular edema, ischemic optic neuropathy; natalizumab → PML risk." },
  { id: 7, question: "What is the first-line medical therapy for idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) and its mechanism?", answer: "Systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitors — acetazolamide (first-line) and methazolamide — which reduce bicarbonate-dependent CSF fluid secretion, lowering intracranial pressure. The IIH Treatment Trial showed acetazolamide (1–4 g/day) plus weight loss reduced ICP and improved papilledema and mild–moderate field defects." },
  { id: 8, question: "List the key adverse effects/cautions of acetazolamide.", answer: "Early alkaline diuresis with Na/K/HCO3 loss → hypokalemia (worse with diuretics/steroids/ACTH; dangerous with digitalis); acid–base risks (can worsen DKA, trigger sickle-cell crisis; caution in COPD and the elderly); kidney stones; common (~50%) paresthesias, malaise, metallic taste, anorexia/weight loss, nausea, somnolence; and rare sulfonamide-type reactions (rash/SJS, thrombocytopenia, aplastic anemia). Avoid in pregnancy." },
  { id: 9, question: "How does methazolamide compare with acetazolamide, and what is topiramate's role in IIH?", answer: "Methazolamide (often BID) may cause less metabolic acidosis/renal bicarbonate loss and fewer stones. Topiramate is a helpful alternative that also lowers ICP and facilitates weight loss, and is a first-line migraine prophylactic — useful when IIH coexists with migraine." },
  { id: 10, question: "Which drugs/substances can cause SECONDARY raised intracranial pressure?", answer: "Vitamin A (>100,000 IU/day), tetracyclines (tetracycline, minocycline, doxycycline), retinoic acid, lithium, and the use of — or withdrawal from — corticosteroids." },
  { id: 11, question: "Why must infection be considered before steroids in orbital/optic nerve inflammation?", answer: "Corticosteroids worsen bacterial, viral (especially herpetic), and fungal infections; in presumed orbital inflammation (which can mimic cellulitis or invasive fungal disease), steroids are withheld until infection is reasonably excluded, since immunosuppression could be catastrophic." },
];

const NEURO_MED4_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Corticosteroids reduce prostaglandins and leukotrienes by:", options: ["Blocking cyclooxygenase only", "Inhibiting arachidonic acid release (phospholipase A2)", "Stimulating lysosomes", "Raising IgG"], correct: 1, explanation: "Steroids inhibit arachidonic acid liberation, cutting all downstream eicosanoids (PGs, LTs, thromboxanes)." },
  { id: 2, question: "A classic OCULAR adverse effect of corticosteroids is:", options: ["Miosis", "Posterior subcapsular cataract and glaucoma", "Optic neuritis", "Keratoconus"], correct: 1, explanation: "Steroids cause PSC cataract, steroid-response glaucoma, infection risk, and central serous chorioretinopathy." },
  { id: 3, question: "Corticosteroids can precipitate which chorioretinal condition?", options: ["Central serous chorioretinopathy", "Retinitis pigmentosa", "Macular hole", "Optic disc drusen"], correct: 0, explanation: "Both systemic and local steroids are associated with central serous chorioretinopathy." },
  { id: 4, question: "The first-line medical therapy for IIH is:", options: ["Topiramate", "Acetazolamide (a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor)", "Furosemide", "Prednisone"], correct: 1, explanation: "Acetazolamide (per the IIHTT) plus weight loss is first-line; topiramate is a useful alternative." },
  { id: 5, question: "A common (~50%) side effect of acetazolamide is:", options: ["Hyperkalemia", "Paresthesias and a metallic taste", "Weight gain", "Mydriasis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Paresthesias, metallic taste (carbonated drinks), malaise, and anorexia are common; kidney stones and hypokalemia also occur." },
  { id: 6, question: "Acetazolamide-induced hypokalemia is especially dangerous in patients taking:", options: ["Digitalis (digoxin)", "Artificial tears", "Antihistamines", "Vitamin C"], correct: 0, explanation: "Hypokalemia potentiates digitalis toxicity; risk rises with concurrent diuretics/steroids." },
  { id: 7, question: "Methazolamide compared with acetazolamide tends to cause:", options: ["More kidney stones", "Less acidosis and fewer stones", "More paresthesias", "No CA inhibition"], correct: 1, explanation: "Methazolamide (often BID) causes less renal bicarbonate loss/acidosis and fewer stones." },
  { id: 8, question: "Which MS disease-modifying drug is associated with macular edema (FAME)?", options: ["Interferon beta", "Fingolimod", "Glatiramer acetate", "Natalizumab"], correct: 1, explanation: "Fingolimod causes macular edema in ~0.4–1% within 3 months, higher with diabetes, uveitis, or recent cataract surgery." },
  { id: 9, question: "Tocilizumab is used as a steroid-sparing biologic in:", options: ["Giant cell arteritis (AAION)", "Cataract", "Dry eye", "Refractive error"], correct: 0, explanation: "Tocilizumab (anti-IL-6R) is used in GCA; NMOSD and MOGAD use rituximab, complement inhibitors, and others." },
  { id: 10, question: "Which drug can cause SECONDARY intracranial hypertension?", options: ["Tetracyclines / high-dose vitamin A / retinoic acid", "Acetazolamide", "Timolol", "Pilocarpine"], correct: 0, explanation: "Tetracyclines, hypervitaminosis A, retinoic acid, lithium, and steroid use/withdrawal can raise ICP." },
  { id: 11, question: "Alemtuzumab (an MS therapy) is notably associated with:", options: ["Graves disease / thyroid eye disease", "Cataract", "Glaucoma", "Keratitis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Alemtuzumab can trigger secondary autoimmune thyroid disease, including thyroid eye disease." },
  { id: 12, question: "Before starting steroids for presumed orbital inflammation, you must:", options: ["Dilate the pupil", "Exclude infection (bacterial cellulitis, invasive fungal disease)", "Check color vision only", "Start antibiotics for everyone"], correct: 1, explanation: "Steroids worsen infection; exclude cellulitis/mucormycosis before immunosuppressing." },
  { id: 13, question: "The mechanism of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors in IIH is to:", options: ["Increase CSF production", "Reduce bicarbonate-dependent CSF fluid secretion", "Block aquaporin-4", "Constrict venous sinuses"], correct: 1, explanation: "CAIs decrease bicarbonate-linked fluid secretion, lowering CSF production and ICP." },
];

// ── Meds · Ch 5 · Botulinum Toxin & Drugs of Abuse
const NEURO_MED5_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is botulinum toxin type A, and how does it work?", answer: "A purified neurotoxin complex produced from Clostridium botulinum (Hall strain). It blocks neuromuscular transmission by binding to motor nerve terminals, entering them, and inhibiting acetylcholine release — producing temporary chemical denervation/weakness of the injected muscle." },
  { id: 2, question: "List the neuro-ophthalmic/ocular uses of botulinum toxin type A.", answer: "Benign essential blepharospasm, hemifacial spasm, and strabismus (it works by atrophic lengthening of the injected extraocular muscle with relative shortening of the antagonist); it is also injected into the levator to induce protective ptosis in facial palsy and used in apraxia of eyelid opening." },
  { id: 3, question: "How does botulinum toxin treat strabismus mechanically?", answer: "Chemical weakening causes atrophic LENGTHENING of the injected (overacting) extraocular muscle while its antagonist relatively SHORTENS, shifting ocular alignment — a temporary, repeatable effect that can realign the eyes or serve as a surgical adjunct." },
  { id: 4, question: "What are the common adverse effects of periocular botulinum toxin?", answer: "Ptosis, local ecchymosis, ectropion, diplopia, lagophthalmos, and exposure keratopathy — usually mild and transient, resolving as the toxin's effect wears off (typically needing reinjection every ~3–4 months)." },
  { id: 5, question: "What PUPIL findings suggest specific drugs of abuse?", answer: "Miosis: opiates. Mydriasis: cocaine, amphetamines, and LSD (and opiate WITHDRAWAL). These pupil clues help identify intoxication in an obtunded or unknown-exposure patient." },
  { id: 6, question: "What eye-movement finding suggests sedative or alcohol use?", answer: "Sustained horizontal gaze-evoked nystagmus (and impaired smooth pursuit) is characteristic of sedative or ethanol intoxication." },
  { id: 7, question: "Describe the optic nerve effects of chronic alcohol use.", answer: "A toxic/nutritional optic neuropathy from direct toxicity and/or associated malnutrition (thiamine, folate, B12 deficiency), causing gradual, painless, bilateral central/cecocentral visual loss." },
  { id: 8, question: "What is Wernicke encephalopathy and its ophthalmic signs?", answer: "An acute thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency syndrome, often in chronic alcoholism, with the triad of ocular signs (ophthalmoplegia — especially CN VI palsies — and nystagmus), confusion/memory disturbance, and ataxia; peripheral neuropathy may coexist. It is reversed/prevented with urgent thiamine." },
  { id: 9, question: "Why is botulinum toxin's effect temporary and requiring reinjection?", answer: "Because the nerve terminal eventually sprouts new functional endings and restores acetylcholine release, chemical denervation wanes over ~3–4 months, so conditions like blepharospasm and hemifacial spasm require repeat injections." },
  { id: 10, question: "Contrast the botulinum dose used for hemifacial spasm versus blepharospasm.", answer: "Hemifacial spasm typically needs a LOWER dose than benign essential blepharospasm (which involves bilateral orbicularis), with reinjection at similar ~3–4-month intervals." },
];

const NEURO_MED5_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Botulinum toxin type A works by:", options: ["Blocking acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction", "Blocking muscarinic receptors", "Stimulating the dilator", "Inhibiting cholinesterase"], correct: 0, explanation: "BoNT-A enters motor terminals and prevents ACh release, causing temporary muscle weakness." },
  { id: 2, question: "Botulinum toxin treats strabismus by:", options: ["Permanently cutting the muscle", "Atrophic lengthening of the injected muscle with relative shortening of the antagonist", "Tightening both muscles", "Blocking the pupil"], correct: 1, explanation: "Chemical weakening lengthens the injected muscle while the antagonist shortens, shifting alignment." },
  { id: 3, question: "A common transient adverse effect of periocular botulinum toxin is:", options: ["Permanent blindness", "Ptosis or diplopia", "Glaucoma", "Cataract"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ptosis, diplopia, ecchymosis, ectropion, and lagophthalmos are usually mild and transient." },
  { id: 4, question: "Pinpoint miosis in an obtunded patient suggests:", options: ["Cocaine", "Opiates", "LSD", "Amphetamine"], correct: 1, explanation: "Opiates cause miosis; cocaine, amphetamines, and LSD (and opiate withdrawal) cause mydriasis." },
  { id: 5, question: "Mydriasis is expected with which substance?", options: ["Heroin", "Cocaine/amphetamines/LSD", "Pilocarpine", "Morphine"], correct: 1, explanation: "Sympathomimetics and hallucinogens (and opiate withdrawal) dilate the pupil; opiate intoxication constricts it." },
  { id: 6, question: "Sustained horizontal gaze-evoked nystagmus suggests intoxication with:", options: ["Opiates", "Sedatives or ethanol", "Cocaine", "LSD"], correct: 1, explanation: "Sedative/alcohol use classically causes gaze-evoked nystagmus and impaired pursuit." },
  { id: 7, question: "The ophthalmic triad of Wernicke encephalopathy is part of:", options: ["Ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus + confusion + ataxia (thiamine deficiency)", "Horner syndrome", "Parinaud syndrome", "Optic neuritis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Wernicke (thiamine deficiency) causes ophthalmoplegia (often CN VI)/nystagmus, confusion, and ataxia — treat with thiamine." },
  { id: 8, question: "Chronic alcohol use can cause a:", options: ["Compressive optic neuropathy", "Toxic/nutritional optic neuropathy (central/cecocentral loss)", "Pigmentary retinopathy", "Retinal detachment"], correct: 1, explanation: "Direct toxicity plus malnutrition (thiamine/folate/B12) causes a painless bilateral central/cecocentral optic neuropathy." },
  { id: 9, question: "Botulinum toxin injections must be repeated roughly every:", options: ["Week", "3–4 months", "5 years", "Day"], correct: 1, explanation: "Nerve terminal sprouting restores function over ~3–4 months, so reinjection is needed for chronic spasm disorders." },
  { id: 10, question: "Botulinum toxin type A is produced from:", options: ["Clostridium tetani", "Clostridium botulinum (Hall strain)", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Streptococcus"], correct: 1, explanation: "BoNT-A is a purified neurotoxin complex from C. botulinum (Hall strain)." },
  { id: 11, question: "The urgent treatment to prevent/reverse Wernicke encephalopathy is:", options: ["Thiamine (vitamin B1)", "Vitamin A", "Folate alone", "Corticosteroids"], correct: 0, explanation: "Immediate thiamine replacement treats and prevents Wernicke encephalopathy." },
  { id: 12, question: "Injecting botulinum toxin into the levator palpebrae is used to:", options: ["Treat glaucoma", "Induce protective ptosis to shield an exposed cornea", "Dilate the pupil", "Correct refractive error"], correct: 1, explanation: "Induced ptosis protects the cornea in severe exposure (e.g., facial palsy) until recovery or definitive surgery." },
];

