// UVEITIS · INFECTIOUS POSTERIOR UVEITIS (CMU · Syphilis/TB/Bartonella/Toxo)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

const UVEITIS_IPOST_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What organism causes syphilis, and why is it called 'the great masquerader'?", answer: "Treponema pallidum (a spirochete). It is 'the great masquerader' because ocular syphilis can involve any structure of the eye (anterior to posterior segment, cranial nerves, pupillomotor pathway) at any stage, mimicking many conditions. Transmission is by sexual contact (acquired) or transplacental (congenital)." },
  { id: 2, question: "Contrast EARLY and LATE congenital syphilis.", answer: "Early (onset <2 years): the most common uveitis form is multifocal chorioretinitis with retinal vasculitis, which may leave a bilateral 'salt-and-pepper' retinopathy; associated with hepatosplenomegaly, long-bone changes, desquamative rash, low birth weight, pneumonia, anemia. Late (>3 years): the Hutchinson triad — interstitial keratitis, CN VIII deafness, and Hutchinson (notched) teeth." },
  { id: 3, question: "Which stages of acquired syphilis cause uveitis, and how common is it?", answer: "Primary: painless chancre (incubation ~3 weeks). Secondary (6–8 weeks later): generalized maculopapular rash on palms/soles, lymphadenopathy, uveitis (~10%). Latent: asymptomatic. Tertiary (⅓ of untreated): cardiovascular syphilis, neurosyphilis, uveitis (~5%). Ocular syphilis is considered NEUROSYPHILIS at any stage." },
  { id: 4, question: "What is the most common ocular inflammation pattern in syphilis?", answer: "Non-granulomatous anterior uveitis is the most common, though it can be granulomatous or non-granulomatous, unilateral or bilateral. The most common posterior finding is focal or multifocal chorioretinitis with vitritis (usually in secondary syphilis)." },
  { id: 5, question: "What is acute syphilitic posterior placoid chorioretinitis (ASPPC)?", answer: "A pathognomonic finding of SECONDARY syphilis: active inflammation of the choriocapillaris–RPE–photoreceptor complex producing a placoid yellow-grey lesion at the RPE level (solitary or multifocal, macular or papillary). FAF shows a hyperautofluorescent lesion; FFA early hypofluorescence with late staining; ICG confluent hypofluorescence (characteristic of syphilitic uveitis).", image: "/uveitis/aspcc-placoid.jpg", imageAlt: "Placoid yellow-grey macular lesion at the RPE level — acute syphilitic posterior placoid chorioretinitis" },
  { id: 6, question: "How is syphilis diagnosed serologically, and what are the test pitfalls?", answer: "Treponemal tests (FTA-ABS, TPHA) are serologic markers (stay positive); non-treponemal tests (VDRL, RPR) track disease activity. False-positive non-treponemal: SLE, pregnancy. False-negative: the prozone effect (dilute the specimen). Congenital syphilis: FTA-ABS IgM test. Lumbar puncture is indicated with neurologic involvement, relapse/retreatment, non-penicillin regimens, and congenital infection." },
  { id: 7, question: "How is ocular syphilis treated?", answer: "As neurosyphilis: IV aqueous penicillin G 18–24 million units/day for 10–14 days (penicillin desensitization if allergic). Do anti-HIV testing and an STD workup. Watch for the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction. Topical/periocular/systemic steroids may be added UNDER antibiotic cover." },
  { id: 8, question: "What organism causes ocular tuberculosis, and what is its typical uveitis pattern?", answer: "Mycobacterium tuberculosis (an acid-fast obligate aerobe), spread by aerosolized droplets. It is a common infectious cause of posterior uveitis in Thailand, a 'great masquerader' causing chronic GRANULOMATOUS inflammation; it can present as anterior, intermediate, or (most commonly) posterior uveitis, plus necrotizing scleritis, phlyctenulosis, and interstitial keratitis." },
  { id: 9, question: "Describe the choroidal manifestations of TB.", answer: "Disseminated choroiditis: multiple (5 to hundreds) deep, discrete, yellowish lesions (choroidal tubercles) at the posterior pole — one of the earliest signs of disseminated disease and more common in the immunocompromised. Serpiginous-like choroiditis is an immune (type IV hypersensitivity) pattern.", image: "/uveitis/tb-serpiginous.jpg", imageAlt: "Serpiginous-like choroiditis / retinal vasculitis in ocular tuberculosis" },
  { id: 10, question: "What is a tuberculoma?", answer: "A single, focal, large (4–14 mm) elevated choroidal mass that may be accompanied by a neurosensory retinal detachment and a macular star — a form of active mycobacterial choroidal infection.", image: "/uveitis/tb-tuberculoma-macular-star.jpg", imageAlt: "Large elevated choroidal tuberculoma with macular star formation" },
  { id: 11, question: "How is TB retinal involvement distinguished from Eales disease?", answer: "TB retinal disease is usually a secondary extension of choroidal disease or an immune response (periphlebitis, often occlusive vasculitis). Eales disease (idiopathic peripheral retinal vasculitis) affects healthy young men 20–40 with recurrent unilateral retinal/vitreous hemorrhage and later fellow-eye involvement — a key differential." },
  { id: 12, question: "What tests support a diagnosis of ocular TB, and what is the intraocular QuantiFERON cutoff?", answer: "The Mantoux tuberculin skin test (TST; intradermal PPD, read induration at 48–72 h — delayed hypersensitivity) and interferon-gamma release assays (IGRA: QuantiFERON-TB Gold In-Tube, T-SPOT). The manufacturer QFT cutoff is 0.35 IU/mL, but for intraocular TB a higher cutoff of 2.0 IU/mL is recommended (sensitivity ~84%, specificity ~87%). Chest imaging (CXR/HRCT) and the COTS guidelines inform management." },
  { id: 13, question: "Outline the treatment of tubercular uveitis.", answer: "Anti-TB therapy for at least 6–9 months (≥9 months recommended for extrapulmonary disease) with the quadruple regimen: isoniazid, rifampicin, ethambutol, and pyrazinamide. Indications to treat (per COTS): recent TB-test conversion, CXR consistent with TB, or positive culture/PCR; corticosteroids/immunosuppression are used sparingly, under cover." },
  { id: 14, question: "Describe Bartonella (cat-scratch) ocular disease.", answer: "Bartonella henselae (a small fastidious gram-negative rod) from cat scratches/licks/bites; flu-like illness with an erythematous papule and lymphadenopathy. Ocular (5–10%): Parinaud oculoglandular syndrome (unilateral granulomatous conjunctivitis + regional lymphadenopathy) and abrupt visual loss with unilateral optic disc edema and a macular star (neuroretinitis) ± focal/multifocal retinitis. The most common posterior finding is a small focal area of retinitis." },
  { id: 15, question: "How is Bartonella diagnosed and treated?", answer: "Clinical features plus serology (a single positive IgG or IgM confirms), with PCR or culture as adjuncts. Often self-limiting; treat with azithromycin (children), doxycycline 100 mg BID for 4–6 weeks (immunocompetent >8 y), IV doxycycline + rifampicin for severe disease, and extended (4-month) therapy in the immunocompromised." },
  { id: 16, question: "Why is Toxoplasma the leading infectious posterior uveitis, and how is it transmitted?", answer: "Toxoplasma gondii is the most common cause of infectious posterior uveitis in adults and children. Felines are the definitive host; humans are intermediate hosts. Forms: oocyst (soil), tachyzoite (infectious), tissue cyst (latent, bradyzoites). Transmission: ingesting cysts in undercooked meat/contaminated food/water/cat feces, congenital transplacental spread, rarely transfusion/transplant." },
  { id: 17, question: "What is the classic appearance of acquired/reactivated ocular toxoplasmosis?", answer: "Focal white retinochoroiditis adjacent to a pigmented chorioretinal scar with overlying vitritis — the 'headlight in the fog' appearance — often with unilateral blur, floaters, ± granulomatous anterior uveitis and raised IOP (~20%). Kyrieleis arteriolitis (segmental arterial plaques) and venous sheathing may be seen.", image: "/uveitis/toxo-headlight.jpg", imageAlt: "Focal white retinochoroiditis beside a pigmented scar with overlying vitritis — toxoplasmosis 'headlight in the fog'" },
  { id: 18, question: "When is ocular toxoplasmosis treated, and with what?", answer: "Treat for immunocompromise, congenital infection, pregnancy, vision-threatening lesions (fovea/optic nerve/major vessels), severe vitritis, lesions >1 disc diameter, prolonged activity (>1 month), or multiple active lesions. Classic triple therapy: pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid (± clindamycin = quadruple); alternatives TMP-SMX, azithromycin, atovaquone. Add corticosteroids 48 h AFTER starting antimicrobials. Pregnancy: spiramycin. Prophylactic TMP-SMX reduces recurrence." },
  { id: 19, question: "What is the classic congenital toxoplasmosis tetrad, and the most common ocular finding?", answer: "Sabin's tetrad: retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus/microcephaly, intracranial calcifications, and cognitive impairment. Congenital transmission occurs in ~40% of primary maternal infections (highest in the 3rd trimester). The most common ocular finding is retinochoroiditis (80%), usually bilateral (85%) and macular; ~25% become blind in one or both eyes." },
];

const UVEITIS_IPOST_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Ocular syphilis at ANY stage should be managed as:", options: ["Localized anterior uveitis", "Neurosyphilis (IV penicillin G)", "A self-limiting infection", "Latent syphilis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ocular syphilis is treated as neurosyphilis with IV aqueous penicillin G 18–24 MU/day for 10–14 days." },
  { id: 2, question: "A placoid yellow-grey macular lesion at the RPE level in secondary syphilis is:", options: ["A choroidal tubercle", "Acute syphilitic posterior placoid chorioretinitis (ASPPC)", "A toxoplasma scar", "A Bartonella macular star"], correct: 1, explanation: "ASPPC is a pathognomonic secondary-syphilis lesion; FAF is hyperautofluorescent and ICG shows confluent hypofluorescence." },
  { id: 3, question: "Which serologic test tracks syphilis disease ACTIVITY (and response to treatment)?", options: ["FTA-ABS", "TPHA", "VDRL/RPR (non-treponemal)", "PCR"], correct: 2, explanation: "Non-treponemal tests (VDRL, RPR) correlate with activity; treponemal tests (FTA-ABS, TPHA) are lifelong markers." },
  { id: 4, question: "A false-NEGATIVE non-treponemal syphilis test can result from the:", options: ["Prozone effect (excess antibody)", "Pregnancy", "SLE", "HIV"], correct: 0, explanation: "The prozone effect (antibody excess) causes false negatives; dilute the specimen. Pregnancy and SLE cause false positives." },
  { id: 5, question: "The Hutchinson triad of LATE congenital syphilis is:", options: ["Interstitial keratitis, CN VIII deafness, Hutchinson teeth", "Chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, calcifications", "Uveitis, cataract, glaucoma", "Rash, fever, lymphadenopathy"], correct: 0, explanation: "Late congenital syphilis: interstitial keratitis, eighth-nerve deafness, and notched Hutchinson teeth." },
  { id: 6, question: "The most common OCULAR inflammation in syphilis is:", options: ["Granulomatous panuveitis", "Non-granulomatous anterior uveitis", "Scleritis", "Optic neuritis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Non-granulomatous anterior uveitis is most common; posterior disease is usually focal/multifocal chorioretinitis with vitritis." },
  { id: 7, question: "Multiple deep discrete yellowish choroidal lesions at the posterior pole in disseminated TB are:", options: ["Choroidal tubercles", "Drusen", "Cotton-wool spots", "Toxoplasma scars"], correct: 0, explanation: "Choroidal tubercles are an early sign of disseminated TB, more common in immunocompromised hosts." },
  { id: 8, question: "A single large (4–14 mm) elevated choroidal mass with a macular star in TB is a:", options: ["Serpiginous choroiditis", "Tuberculoma", "Choroidal tubercle", "Phlyctenule"], correct: 1, explanation: "A tuberculoma is a focal elevated choroidal mass that may have a neurosensory detachment and macular star." },
  { id: 9, question: "For INTRAOCULAR TB, the recommended QuantiFERON-TB Gold cutoff is:", options: ["0.35 IU/mL", "2.0 IU/mL", "10 IU/mL", "Any positive"], correct: 1, explanation: "Although the manufacturer cutoff is 0.35 IU/mL, a higher 2.0 IU/mL cutoff is recommended for intraocular TB (sens ~84%, spec ~87%)." },
  { id: 10, question: "The standard quadruple anti-TB regimen is:", options: ["Isoniazid, rifampicin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide", "Penicillin, doxycycline, rifampicin, azithromycin", "Pyrimethamine, sulfadiazine, folinic acid, clindamycin", "Ganciclovir, foscarnet, cidofovir, acyclovir"], correct: 0, explanation: "HREZ (isoniazid, rifampicin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide) for ≥6–9 months (≥9 for extrapulmonary) treats tubercular uveitis." },
  { id: 11, question: "TB peripheral occlusive retinal vasculitis must be differentiated from:", options: ["Eales disease", "Behçet disease", "Sarcoidosis", "VKH"], correct: 0, explanation: "Eales disease (idiopathic peripheral retinal vasculitis in young men) is the classic differential for TB retinal vasculitis." },
  { id: 12, question: "Parinaud oculoglandular syndrome (granulomatous conjunctivitis + regional lymphadenopathy) is classically caused by:", options: ["Toxoplasma gondii", "Bartonella henselae (cat-scratch)", "Treponema pallidum", "M. tuberculosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Bartonella henselae causes Parinaud oculoglandular syndrome and neuroretinitis with a macular star." },
  { id: 13, question: "Neuroretinitis with abrupt visual loss, disc edema, and a macular star suggests:", options: ["Bartonella (cat-scratch)", "CMV retinitis", "ARN", "Syphilitic ASPPC"], correct: 0, explanation: "Cat-scratch (Bartonella) neuroretinitis presents with disc edema and a macular star of exudates." },
  { id: 14, question: "The most common cause of infectious posterior uveitis overall is:", options: ["Tuberculosis", "Syphilis", "Toxoplasmosis", "Bartonella"], correct: 2, explanation: "Toxoplasma gondii retinochoroiditis is the most common infectious posterior uveitis in adults and children." },
  { id: 15, question: "'Headlight in the fog' describes:", options: ["A placoid RPE lesion", "Focal retinochoroiditis adjacent to a pigmented scar with overlying vitritis", "A choroidal tubercle", "A macular star"], correct: 1, explanation: "The toxoplasmosis 'headlight in the fog' is an active white retinochoroiditis next to an old pigmented scar, seen through vitreous haze." },
  { id: 16, question: "In ocular toxoplasmosis, corticosteroids should be started:", options: ["Before antimicrobials", "48 hours AFTER starting antimicrobials", "Instead of antimicrobials", "Never"], correct: 1, explanation: "Steroids are added ~48 h after antiparasitic cover to avoid unchecked proliferation, then tapered." },
  { id: 17, question: "The classic triple therapy for toxoplasmosis is:", options: ["Pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid", "Isoniazid + rifampicin + ethambutol", "Penicillin + doxycycline + steroid", "Ganciclovir + foscarnet + steroid"], correct: 0, explanation: "Pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid is classic; adding clindamycin makes it quadruple. TMP-SMX is an alternative." },
  { id: 18, question: "Segmental arterial plaques (Kyrieleis arteriolitis) with venous sheathing are seen in:", options: ["Toxoplasmosis", "Syphilitic ASPPC", "CMV retinitis", "Sarcoidosis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Kyrieleis arteriolitis (segmental periarterial plaques) is a recognized feature of active toxoplasmic retinochoroiditis." },
  { id: 19, question: "The classic congenital toxoplasmosis tetrad (Sabin) includes all EXCEPT:", options: ["Retinochoroiditis", "Intracranial calcifications", "Hydrocephalus/microcephaly", "Interstitial keratitis"], correct: 3, explanation: "Sabin's tetrad: retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus/microcephaly, intracranial calcifications, cognitive impairment. Interstitial keratitis is congenital SYPHILIS." },
];

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// UVEITIS · NON-INFECTIOUS UVEITIS & SCLERITIS (CMU · "All About Uveitis")
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

const UVEITIS_NONINF_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada (VKH) disease and its hallmark fundus sign?", answer: "A bilateral granulomatous panuveitis (an autoimmune process against melanocytes) affecting the eyes, meninges, ears, and skin. The hallmark chronic/convalescent sign is a 'sunset glow fundus' (depigmentation), with integumentary signs of vitiligo, poliosis, and alopecia.", image: "/uveitis/vkh-sunset-glow.jpg", imageAlt: "Diffuse choroidal depigmentation (sunset-glow pattern) on widefield fundus in VKH" },
  { id: 2, question: "Contrast the treatment of acute vs chronic VKH.", answer: "Acute VKH: IV methylprednisolone 1 g/day for 3 days, then oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day with slow taper over ~9 months; systemic immunosuppression is usually required. Chronic VKH: oral prednisolone 0.5–1 mg/kg/day slow taper, systemic immunosuppressants usually required, plus management of complications." },
  { id: 3, question: "What is sympathetic ophthalmia (SO)?", answer: "A bilateral, diffuse, granulomatous, non-necrotizing panuveitis that develops after surgical or accidental penetrating trauma to ONE eye and then inflames the fellow (sympathizing) eye. Incidence ~0.01% after surgery, ~0.5% after trauma; pars plana vitrectomy is now a leading risk. About 1/3 develop SO within 3 months and 1/2 within 1 year." },
  { id: 4, question: "How does SO differ histologically from VKH, and what is the role of enucleation?", answer: "SO clinically resembles VKH but histology SPARES the choriocapillaris in the early stage (VKH involves it). Enucleation of a blind, disorganized exciting eye within 2 weeks of injury may reduce SO risk — but this is reconsidered and reserved only for a hopelessly disorganized globe." },
  { id: 5, question: "Describe ocular sarcoidosis.", answer: "A multisystem granulomatous inflammation of unknown etiology; pulmonary involvement is most common (90%) and ocular involvement occurs in ~50% (uveitis most common). Histology: non-caseating granulomas. Retinal periphlebitis gives irregular nodular perivenous granulomas — 'candle-wax dripping' (taches de bougie)." },
  { id: 6, question: "How is ocular sarcoidosis diagnosed?", answer: "No gold standard. Support: CXR/HRCT (high sensitivity for hilar adenopathy), elevated serum ACE and lysozyme, and histopathology from tissue biopsy. International Workshop on Ocular Sarcoidosis criteria: definite (biopsy-proven), presumed (clinical + bilateral hilar adenopathy), probable (ancillary tests)." },
  { id: 7, question: "Describe Behçet disease and its ocular features.", answer: "A chronic relapsing systemic occlusive vasculitis (Old Silk Road distribution, more common in men); ~80% bilateral. Eye: non-granulomatous anterior uveitis with a MOBILE hypopyon and panuveitis with occlusive NECROTIZING retinal vasculitis. Complications: glaucoma, optic atrophy, CME, neovascularization, cataract." },
  { id: 8, question: "What is the FFA appearance and treatment of Behçet uveitis?", answer: "FFA shows typical diffuse capillary leakage in a 'fern-like' pattern. Treatment: oral steroids plus immunomodulatory therapy are required; EULAR recommends azathioprine first-line, with ciclosporin, infliximab, or adalimumab as second-line." },
  { id: 9, question: "Describe tattoo-associated uveitis.", answer: "A bilateral granulomatous inflammation (a type IV delayed hypersensitivity) that mimics sarcoidosis, typically beginning ≥6 months after tattooing, common in young people; chronic anterior uveitis is the most common presentation. Induration and scaling of the skin tattoo accompanying eye inflammation is the diagnostic clue. Treat with systemic steroids plus immunomodulatory therapy." },
  { id: 10, question: "What are the cardinal clinical features of scleritis?", answer: "Inflammation of the sclera with deep, boring ocular pain (worse at night), globe tenderness, redness, and swelling; vision drops if the cornea or posterior segment is involved. A violaceous hue from scleral/episcleral edema and dilated DEEP episcleral plexus that does NOT blanch with topical phenylephrine (distinguishing it from episcleritis). ~40% are associated with a systemic disease." },
  { id: 11, question: "Give the classification of scleritis and the most common type.", answer: "Anterior vs posterior; infectious vs non-infectious (immune-mediated). Anterior non-infectious: non-necrotizing (diffuse or nodular) and necrotizing (with inflammation, or without inflammation = scleromalacia perforans). Diffuse anterior scleritis is the MOST common and least severe; nodular carries ~10% risk of turning necrotizing." },
  { id: 12, question: "Which systemic diseases are most associated with scleritis (especially necrotizing)?", answer: "Rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA), SLE, relapsing polychondritis, and polyarteritis nodosa. Necrotizing scleritis with inflammation is associated with systemic disease in up to ~50% (granulomatous or occlusive-vasculitic). Scleromalacia perforans (necrotizing WITHOUT inflammation) is classic in older patients with long-standing RA." },
  { id: 13, question: "Describe posterior scleritis and its key imaging sign.", answer: "Deep eye pain and decreased vision, often WITHOUT anterior inflammatory signs; features include choroidal folds, disc swelling, and exudative retinal detachment. The classic B-scan finding is the 'T-sign' (fluid in Tenon's space around the optic nerve)." },
  { id: 14, question: "Outline the management of scleritis.", answer: "Find the associated systemic disease (work up every case; up to 30–50% have a systemic association), control ocular inflammation, relieve discomfort, and prevent complications. Treatment ladder: oral NSAIDs (naproxen, indomethacin, ibuprofen, COX-2), topical/oral steroids, and immunosuppressive agents for necrotizing or refractory disease (with a pre-steroid work-up)." },
  { id: 15, question: "List complications associated with scleritis.", answer: "Peripheral keratitis (up to 37%), scleral thinning (up to 33%), uveitis (up to 30%), glaucoma (up to 18%), and cataract (up to 7%)." },
  { id: 16, question: "What distinguishes episcleritis from scleritis on exam?", answer: "In episcleritis the superficial episcleral vessels blanch with topical phenylephrine and pain is mild; in scleritis the deep episcleral plexus does NOT blanch, the redness has a violaceous hue, and pain is severe and boring (worse at night)." },
  { id: 17, question: "Why is early recognition of VKH and prompt high-dose steroids important?", answer: "Early aggressive corticosteroid therapy (with immunosuppression) in acute VKH prevents progression to the chronic 'sunset glow' depigmentation stage and its complications, improving the chance of preserving vision." },
];

const UVEITIS_NONINF_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "A 'sunset glow fundus' with vitiligo and poliosis is characteristic of:", options: ["Behçet disease", "Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada disease", "Sarcoidosis", "Sympathetic ophthalmia"], correct: 1, explanation: "VKH is a bilateral granulomatous panuveitis against melanocytes; the chronic stage shows a sunset glow fundus with poliosis/vitiligo/alopecia." },
  { id: 2, question: "Acute VKH is treated with:", options: ["Observation", "IV methylprednisolone then oral prednisolone taper over ~9 months ± immunosuppression", "Topical steroid only", "Antivirals"], correct: 1, explanation: "Acute VKH needs IV methylprednisolone 1 g/day ×3 then a slow oral taper over ~9 months, usually with immunosuppressants." },
  { id: 3, question: "Sympathetic ophthalmia develops after:", options: ["A viral infection", "Penetrating trauma or surgery to one eye", "Corticosteroid use", "A tattoo"], correct: 1, explanation: "SO is a bilateral granulomatous panuveitis of the fellow eye after penetrating trauma/surgery (PPV is now a leading risk)." },
  { id: 4, question: "Histologically, sympathetic ophthalmia differs from VKH early by:", options: ["Involving the choriocapillaris", "SPARING the choriocapillaris", "Caseating granulomas", "Absence of granulomas"], correct: 1, explanation: "SO spares the choriocapillaris in the early stage, whereas VKH involves it — a key histologic distinction (Dalen-Fuchs nodules are seen in both)." },
  { id: 5, question: "'Candle-wax dripping' (perivenous nodular granulomas) is characteristic of:", options: ["Behçet", "Sarcoidosis", "VKH", "Toxoplasmosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Sarcoid retinal periphlebitis produces 'candle-wax drippings' (taches de bougie) along venules." },
  { id: 6, question: "The histopathologic hallmark of sarcoidosis is:", options: ["Caseating granulomas", "Non-caseating granulomas", "Fibrinoid necrosis", "Amyloid deposits"], correct: 1, explanation: "Sarcoidosis shows non-caseating granulomatous inflammation; CXR/HRCT, ACE, and lysozyme support the diagnosis." },
  { id: 7, question: "A MOBILE hypopyon with occlusive retinal vasculitis in a young man suggests:", options: ["Sarcoidosis", "Behçet disease", "VKH", "Syphilis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Behçet causes a non-granulomatous AU with a shifting (mobile) hypopyon and occlusive necrotizing retinal vasculitis." },
  { id: 8, question: "First-line immunomodulatory therapy for Behçet uveitis (per EULAR) is:", options: ["Azathioprine", "Methotrexate", "Cyclophosphamide", "Rituximab"], correct: 0, explanation: "EULAR recommends azathioprine first-line; ciclosporin, infliximab, or adalimumab are second-line for Behçet." },
  { id: 9, question: "A granulomatous uveitis mimicking sarcoidosis that began 6 months after body art, with skin induration, is:", options: ["Tattoo-associated uveitis", "VKH", "Behçet", "Scleritis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Tattoo-associated uveitis is a type IV hypersensitivity granulomatous uveitis; tattoo induration/scaling is the clue." },
  { id: 10, question: "Which exam finding distinguishes scleritis from episcleritis?", options: ["Redness blanches with phenylephrine", "Deep vessels do NOT blanch with phenylephrine; severe boring pain", "Mild itching", "Follicular reaction"], correct: 1, explanation: "Scleral (deep plexus) redness does not blanch with phenylephrine and causes severe night-worse pain, unlike episcleritis." },
  { id: 11, question: "The most common type of scleritis is:", options: ["Necrotizing with inflammation", "Diffuse anterior (non-necrotizing)", "Scleromalacia perforans", "Posterior scleritis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Diffuse anterior scleritis is the most common and least severe; nodular carries ~10% risk of becoming necrotizing." },
  { id: 12, question: "Necrotizing scleritis WITHOUT inflammation (scleromalacia perforans) is classically associated with:", options: ["Long-standing rheumatoid arthritis", "Behçet disease", "Syphilis", "Sarcoidosis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Scleromalacia perforans occurs in older patients with long-standing RA; the sclera thins painlessly without overt inflammation." },
  { id: 13, question: "The B-scan 'T-sign' indicates:", options: ["Anterior scleritis", "Posterior scleritis (fluid in Tenon's space)", "Retinal detachment", "Choroidal melanoma"], correct: 1, explanation: "The T-sign (peri-optic nerve fluid in Tenon's space) is classic for posterior scleritis." },
  { id: 14, question: "Which systemic disease is most associated with necrotizing scleritis?", options: ["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) / rheumatoid arthritis", "Diabetes", "Hypertension", "Migraine"], correct: 0, explanation: "RA, GPA, SLE, relapsing polychondritis, and PAN are the key associations; necrotizing scleritis especially flags systemic vasculitis." },
  { id: 15, question: "First-line medical therapy for non-necrotizing scleritis is often:", options: ["Oral NSAIDs", "IV cyclophosphamide", "Topical antibiotics", "Antivirals"], correct: 0, explanation: "Oral NSAIDs (naproxen, indomethacin) are first-line; steroids and immunosuppression are escalated for necrotizing/refractory disease." },
  { id: 16, question: "Posterior scleritis may present with all EXCEPT:", options: ["Choroidal folds", "Exudative retinal detachment", "A blanching superficial redness with mild pain", "Disc swelling"], correct: 2, explanation: "Posterior scleritis causes deep pain, choroidal folds, disc swelling, and exudative RD — not a mild blanching episcleral redness." },
  { id: 17, question: "A leading modern risk factor for sympathetic ophthalmia is:", options: ["Pars plana vitrectomy", "Cataract drops", "Contact lens wear", "LASIK"], correct: 0, explanation: "Beyond accidental trauma, repeated vitreoretinal surgery (PPV) is now a leading risk for SO." },
  { id: 18, question: "A bilateral granulomatous panuveitis with meningismus, tinnitus, and later poliosis is:", options: ["VKH", "Behçet", "Sarcoid", "Scleritis"], correct: 0, explanation: "VKH involves eyes, meninges (meningismus/headache), ears (tinnitus/hearing loss), and skin (poliosis/vitiligo/alopecia)." },
];

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// UVEITIS · VIRAL & ANTERIOR UVEITIS (CMU · "All About Uveitis")
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

const UVEITIS_VIRAL_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Which viruses cause viral anterior uveitis, and what is the classic clinical triad?", answer: "CMV, VZV, HSV, and rubella (also HHV, EBV). The classic picture is a UNILATERAL anterior uveitis with raised IOP, granulomatous keratic precipitates (± corneal scars), and often iris atrophy. Fuchs uveitis syndrome and Posner-Schlossman syndrome sit within this spectrum.", image: "/uveitis/viral-kp.jpg", imageAlt: "Keratic precipitates on the corneal endothelium in viral anterior uveitis" },
  { id: 2, question: "How do the keratic precipitates help identify the causative virus?", answer: "HSV/VZV: small-to-medium KPs; VZV causes sectoral iris atrophy, HSV causes patchy/disciform scars. CMV: 'coin-shaped' KPs (round, nodular) and linear KPs, with diffuse ('moth-eaten') iris atrophy and endothelial cell loss. Rubella (Fuchs): diffuse fine-to-medium stellate KPs scattered over the whole endothelium." },
  { id: 3, question: "Describe CMV anterior uveitis / endotheliitis.", answer: "A spectrum: acute presentation as Posner-Schlossman syndrome, chronic as Fuchs-like uveitis or corneal endotheliitis ± mild chronic anterior uveitis. Usually unilateral, male-predominant, older age, with raised IOP, low-grade AU, coin-shaped KPs, and diffuse iris stromal atrophy. The 'owl's eye' cytomegalic inclusion is the histologic hallmark.", image: "/uveitis/cmv-iris-atrophy.jpg", imageAlt: "Diffuse iris stromal atrophy in CMV anterior uveitis" },
  { id: 4, question: "How is CMV anterior uveitis diagnosed and treated?", answer: "Diagnosis: real-time PCR of aqueous, the Goldmann-Witmer coefficient, and corneal confocal microscopy. Treatment: systemic ganciclovir/valganciclovir, topical ganciclovir gel/drops, or a ganciclovir implant, plus topical steroid — up to ~75% respond, but recurrence is common (33–75%)." },
  { id: 5, question: "Describe Posner-Schlossman syndrome (glaucomatocyclitic crisis).", answer: "Recurrent acute attacks of MILD unilateral anterior uveitis with only a few (granulomatous) KPs but MARKEDLY elevated IOP (high prostaglandin levels). CMV is a major aetiology; male predominant (50–70%), age 20–40, secondary glaucoma up to 26%, and generally good prognosis once IOP is controlled." },
  { id: 6, question: "Describe Fuchs uveitis syndrome (FUS).", answer: "A chronic, low-grade anterior uveitis — commonly rubella (Western) or CMV (Eastern), also toxoplasmosis/HSV. Features: iris heterochromia, diffuse fine-to-medium stellate KPs over the whole endothelium, posterior subcapsular cataract, and a characteristic LACK of posterior synechiae. It responds poorly to steroids.", image: "/uveitis/fuchs-heterochromia.jpg", imageAlt: "Iris heterochromia with stellate keratic precipitates in Fuchs uveitis syndrome" },
  { id: 7, question: "What are the other signs and complications of Fuchs uveitis syndrome?", answer: "Abnormal fragile angle vessels giving the Amsler sign (filiform hemorrhage on AC paracentesis/intraocular surgery), Koeppe nodules (20–30%), vitritis (common in rubella-associated FUS), and secondary glaucoma (~25%). Cataract surgery is generally well tolerated despite the chronic inflammation." },
  { id: 8, question: "Compare the pattern of iris atrophy among HSV, VZV, and CMV anterior uveitis.", answer: "HSV: patchy/localized iris atrophy (± disciform keratitis/scar). VZV: SECTORAL iris atrophy (plus HZO dermatomal rash). CMV: DIFFUSE ('moth-eaten') iris atrophy with endothelial cell loss and coin-shaped KPs." },
  { id: 9, question: "Describe CMV retinitis and its at-risk population.", answer: "The most common ocular infection in HIV patients (CD4 usually <50 cells/µL; screen at <100), though it also occurs in other immunocompromised and occasionally healthy hosts. Forms: classic (necrotizing with hemorrhage — 'pizza/brush-fire'), granular (indolent peripheral), and frosted-branch angiitis. Vision loss from retinal detachment, macular scar, or optic neuropathy." },
  { id: 10, question: "How is CMV retinitis treated?", answer: "Induction IV ganciclovir 5 mg/kg q12h for 14 days (monitor CBC), then maintenance 5 mg/kg q24h until inactive; or intravitreal ganciclovir 2 mg every 1–2 weeks. Other anti-CMV agents: valganciclovir (oral), foscarnet and cidofovir (IV). Restore immune function (antiretrovirals)." },
  { id: 11, question: "Describe necrotizing herpetic retinitis — ARN and PORN.", answer: "Acute retinal necrosis (ARN): peripheral confluent full-thickness retinal necrosis, occlusive arteriolitis, and vitritis in immunocompetent or mildly immunocompromised patients (VZV > HSV). Progressive outer retinal necrosis (PORN): rapid posterior outer-retinal necrosis with minimal vitritis in profoundly immunocompromised (AIDS) patients (VZV). Most common organism overall: VZV > HSV > CMV > EBV." },
  { id: 12, question: "How is necrotizing herpetic retinitis (ARN/PORN) treated?", answer: "Induction IV acyclovir 10–15 mg/kg q8h for 14 days (monitor renal function), then oral acyclovir or valacyclovir for ~3 months; intravitreal ganciclovir/foscarnet is added for posterior/aggressive disease. Aqueous PCR confirms the organism. Watch for rhegmatogenous retinal detachment." },
  { id: 13, question: "What is the 'headlight in the fog' vs necrotizing retinitis distinction on exam?", answer: "Toxoplasmosis gives a focal white retinochoroiditis next to a pigmented scar with dense overlying vitritis ('headlight in the fog'). Necrotizing herpetic retinitis (ARN) gives peripheral confluent full-thickness necrosis with occlusive vasculitis ('crack-mud' appearance) and is a viral (VZV/HSV) emergency." },
  { id: 14, question: "Why do Fuchs uveitis and Posner-Schlossman NOT form posterior synechiae?", answer: "Both are low-grade, chronic/recurrent viral anterior uveitides with minimal fibrinous exudation, so iris-to-lens adhesions (posterior synechiae) characteristically do NOT form — a useful clue distinguishing them from other anterior uveitides." },
  { id: 15, question: "Which anterior uveitis signs should raise suspicion for CMV specifically?", answer: "Coin-shaped (round nodular) KPs, linear KPs, diffuse iris atrophy with endothelial cell loss/corneal edema, raised IOP with low-grade inflammation, in an older male — a picture suggesting CMV anterior uveitis/endotheliitis (confirm with aqueous PCR)." },
  { id: 16, question: "What FDA-approved anti-CMV drugs exist by route?", answer: "Oral: ganciclovir, valganciclovir. IV: ganciclovir, cidofovir, foscarnet. Intravitreal: ganciclovir (including the implant) and fomivirsen." },
  { id: 17, question: "What is the significance of raised IOP in viral anterior uveitis?", answer: "Trabeculitis from viral (herpetic/CMV) inflammation raises IOP out of proportion to the low-grade cellular reaction — a hallmark of viral AU (and of Posner-Schlossman). It often parallels inflammatory activity and needs IOP-lowering therapy plus antiviral/steroid treatment." },
];

const UVEITIS_VIRAL_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "A unilateral anterior uveitis with raised IOP, granulomatous KPs, and iris atrophy most suggests:", options: ["HLA-B27 uveitis", "Viral (herpetic/CMV) anterior uveitis", "Sarcoid uveitis", "Juvenile idiopathic arthritis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Unilateral AU with high IOP, granulomatous KPs, and iris atrophy is the classic viral anterior uveitis picture." },
  { id: 2, question: "Coin-shaped (round nodular) keratic precipitates with diffuse iris atrophy suggest which virus?", options: ["HSV", "VZV", "CMV", "Rubella"], correct: 2, explanation: "Coin-shaped/linear KPs and diffuse 'moth-eaten' iris atrophy with endothelial loss point to CMV anterior uveitis." },
  { id: 3, question: "SECTORAL iris atrophy is most characteristic of:", options: ["CMV", "VZV", "Rubella", "Toxoplasma"], correct: 1, explanation: "VZV causes sectoral iris atrophy (CMV = diffuse; HSV = patchy)." },
  { id: 4, question: "Recurrent mild unilateral AU with few KPs but MARKEDLY high IOP is:", options: ["Fuchs uveitis syndrome", "Posner-Schlossman syndrome", "VKH", "Behçet"], correct: 1, explanation: "Posner-Schlossman (glaucomatocyclitic crisis) has mild inflammation with very high IOP; CMV is a major cause." },
  { id: 5, question: "Iris heterochromia, diffuse stellate KPs, PSC, and NO posterior synechiae describe:", options: ["Fuchs uveitis syndrome", "CMV retinitis", "Sarcoidosis", "ARN"], correct: 0, explanation: "Fuchs uveitis syndrome: chronic low-grade AU with heterochromia, stellate KPs, PSC, and classically no posterior synechiae." },
  { id: 6, question: "The Amsler sign (filiform AC hemorrhage on paracentesis) is seen in:", options: ["Behçet", "Fuchs uveitis syndrome", "VKH", "Toxoplasmosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Fragile abnormal angle vessels in FUS bleed on AC entry — the Amsler sign." },
  { id: 7, question: "CMV anterior uveitis is confirmed by:", options: ["Serum IgG", "Aqueous real-time PCR / Goldmann-Witmer coefficient", "Chest CT", "Skin test"], correct: 1, explanation: "Aqueous PCR and the Goldmann-Witmer coefficient confirm CMV anterior uveitis; corneal confocal may show owl's-eye cells." },
  { id: 8, question: "CMV retinitis typically occurs in HIV patients when the CD4 count is below:", options: ["500", "200", "50", "350"], correct: 2, explanation: "CMV retinitis classically occurs at CD4 <50 cells/µL; screening is recommended at <100." },
  { id: 9, question: "Induction therapy for CMV retinitis is:", options: ["IV acyclovir", "IV ganciclovir 5 mg/kg q12h for 14 days", "Oral valacyclovir", "Topical ganciclovir"], correct: 1, explanation: "IV ganciclovir induction (5 mg/kg q12h × 14 days) then maintenance; intravitreal ganciclovir is an alternative." },
  { id: 10, question: "The most common organism in acute retinal necrosis (ARN) is:", options: ["CMV", "VZV", "Toxoplasma", "HSV-1"], correct: 1, explanation: "VZV is the most common cause of necrotizing herpetic retinitis (VZV > HSV > CMV > EBV)." },
  { id: 11, question: "Progressive outer retinal necrosis (PORN) occurs characteristically in:", options: ["Healthy young adults", "Profoundly immunocompromised (AIDS) patients", "Diabetics", "Children"], correct: 1, explanation: "PORN is a rapidly progressive VZV outer retinitis with minimal vitritis in severely immunocompromised patients." },
  { id: 12, question: "Induction treatment for ARN is:", options: ["IV ganciclovir", "IV acyclovir 10–15 mg/kg q8h for 14 days then oral antiviral", "Oral prednisolone", "Intravitreal triamcinolone"], correct: 1, explanation: "IV acyclovir induction then oral acyclovir/valacyclovir for ~3 months; add intravitreal antiviral for aggressive disease." },
  { id: 13, question: "Fuchs uveitis syndrome characteristically responds to steroids:", options: ["Dramatically", "Poorly", "Only with high dose", "Not applicable"], correct: 1, explanation: "FUS is a low-grade, poorly steroid-responsive uveitis; over-treatment with steroids risks cataract/glaucoma without benefit." },
  { id: 14, question: "Raised IOP out of proportion to mild inflammation in viral AU reflects:", options: ["Angle closure", "Trabeculitis", "Steroid response only", "Hyphema"], correct: 1, explanation: "Viral (herpetic/CMV) trabeculitis raises IOP out of proportion to the low-grade cell, often paralleling inflammatory activity." },
  { id: 15, question: "An intravitreal FDA-approved anti-CMV agent is:", options: ["Acyclovir", "Ganciclovir (incl. implant) / fomivirsen", "Penicillin", "Pyrimethamine"], correct: 1, explanation: "Intravitreal ganciclovir (and the implant) and fomivirsen are FDA-approved for CMV; foscarnet/cidofovir are IV options." },
  { id: 16, question: "Lack of posterior synechiae despite chronic AU is typical of:", options: ["Fuchs uveitis syndrome and Posner-Schlossman", "Sarcoidosis", "Behçet", "VKH"], correct: 0, explanation: "Both FUS and Posner-Schlossman produce minimal fibrin, so posterior synechiae characteristically do not form." },
  { id: 17, question: "A unilateral anterior uveitis in an older man with high IOP, coin-shaped KPs, and corneal edema should prompt testing for:", options: ["CMV (aqueous PCR)", "HLA-B27", "ANA", "Syphilis only"], correct: 0, explanation: "This is the classic CMV anterior uveitis/endotheliitis picture — confirm with aqueous CMV PCR." },
  { id: 18, question: "The 'crack-mud' confluent peripheral retinal necrosis with occlusive arteriolitis describes:", options: ["Toxoplasmosis", "Acute retinal necrosis (ARN)", "CMV retinitis granular form", "Serpiginous choroiditis"], correct: 1, explanation: "ARN shows confluent full-thickness peripheral necrosis ('crack-mud'), occlusive arteriolitis, and vitritis — a viral emergency." },
];

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// UVEITIS · COMPLICATIONS OF UVEITIS (A. Apivatthakakul · CMU)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

const UVEITIS_COMPLIC_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is the overall burden of uveitis complications?", answer: "Uveitis causes 10–20% permanent visual loss and is the 4th leading cause of blindness in developed countries, most common in adults 20–60. Complications (not the inflammation itself) cause most of the vision loss." },
  { id: 2, question: "Describe calcific band keratopathy in uveitis.", answer: "Deposition of calcium in the corneal epithelial basement membrane and Bowman layer in the INTERPALPEBRAL zone, from chronic ocular inflammation (and systemic hypercalcemia). It advances horizontally from the limbus to center with a LUCID interval at the limbus and a 'Swiss-cheese' appearance (holes where nerves penetrate). Symptoms: irritation, tearing, glare, photophobia, reduced vision." },
  { id: 3, question: "How is band keratopathy treated?", answer: "Treat the underlying cause; for visual disturbance or irritation: mechanical debridement/surgical keratectomy, EDTA chelation (0.05 mol/L), or phototherapeutic keratectomy (PTK)." },
  { id: 4, question: "Why is cataract the leading complication of chronic uveitis, and what type?", answer: "Prolonged breakdown of the blood-ocular barrier lets plasma phospholipids enter the eye, increasing lens epithelial permeability → posterior subcapsular cataract (the most common type). Cataract accounts for up to 40% of visual loss in uveitis and is the most common indication for surgery." },
  { id: 5, question: "What are the timing rules and IOL principles for cataract surgery in uveitis?", answer: "The eye must be QUIESCENT for at least 3 months pre-op and controlled throughout the post-op period (balance against amblyopia risk in children). Prefer a single-piece acrylic PCIOL in the bag; AVOID silicone material, sulcus and AC IOLs, and defer IOL implantation in poorly controlled uveitis. Pre-treat with topical/systemic steroids (± antivirals for herpetic eyes)." },
  { id: 6, question: "What proportion of uveitis patients develop ocular hypertension/glaucoma, and which uveitides are high-risk?", answer: "About 10–20%. High-risk entities: Fuchs heterochromic iridocyclitis, Posner-Schlossman syndrome, herpetic uveitis, juvenile idiopathic arthritis, and sarcoidosis." },
  { id: 7, question: "Give the clinical classification of uveitic ocular hypertension/glaucoma.", answer: "(1) Inflammatory ocular hypertension syndrome (IOHS, trabeculitis — IOP parallels inflammation); (2) acute uveitic angle closure (pupillary block from posterior synechiae/iris bombé, or non-pupillary block from forward lens-iris movement/choroidal effusion); (3) corticosteroid-induced ocular hypertension; (4) chronic mixed-mechanism glaucoma (PAS, chronic trabecular damage)." },
  { id: 8, question: "Describe steroid-induced ocular hypertension.", answer: "Occurs in ~1/3 of patients, typically 2–6 weeks after starting corticosteroids; depends on dose, frequency, compound, route, duration, and susceptibility. Steroid responders include those with POAG, rheumatoid arthritis, extremes of age, and diabetes." },
  { id: 9, question: "Outline the medical management of uveitic glaucoma.", answer: "First line: beta-blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, alpha-agonists. Optional: hyperosmotic agents, prostaglandin analogues (with caution — may worsen inflammation/CME). MIOTICS ARE CONTRAINDICATED (worsen inflammation and synechiae). Treat the underlying inflammation. Surgery (trabeculectomy ± antimetabolite, glaucoma drainage device, cyclo-ablation) for uncontrolled IOP." },
  { id: 10, question: "Describe hypotony in uveitis.", answer: "IOP <6 mmHg in ~10% of uveitis patients (especially children). Acute: ciliary body inflammation shuts down aqueous production. Chronic: ciliary body atrophy and a cyclitic membrane cause tractional ciliary detachment → permanent hypotony with hypotony maculopathy, optic nerve edema, choroidal folds, and eventually phthisis bulbi (poor prognosis)." },
  { id: 11, question: "Describe uveitic cystoid macular edema (CME) and its mechanism.", answer: "Multiple cyst-like fluid pockets in the macula (largest centrally) from leakage across the inner/outer blood-retinal barrier into the outer plexiform layer, driven by inflammatory cytokines (VEGF, IL-6) ± vitreomacular traction. FFA shows a petalloid perifoveal leakage pattern. It is a leading cause of vision loss in uveitis." },
  { id: 12, question: "How is uveitic CME treated?", answer: "Treat the primary cause, then: NSAIDs (inhibit COX/PG), corticosteroids (topical, sub-Tenon, intravitreal triamcinolone, or implant, ± systemic), acetazolamide (a CAI reduces RPE leakage), anti-VEGF (in controlled inflammation), and immunomodulatory drugs (lower side-effect burden than chronic steroids)." },
  { id: 13, question: "Classify retinal detachment in uveitis by mechanism.", answer: "Exudative (VKH, sympathetic ophthalmia, uveal effusion syndrome), tractional (toxocariasis, Eales disease), and rhegmatogenous (viral retinitis — poor visual prognosis, high PVR risk ~30%)." },
  { id: 14, question: "What drives retinal/choroidal neovascularization in uveitis, and how is it treated?", answer: "Retinal ischemia → pro-angiogenic cytokines (VEGF) → retinal NV; chronic ocular inflammation (IL-1β, TNF-α, IL-6, IL-8) → pathologic angiogenesis; recurrent choroiditis → CNV. Treat the primary disease/inflammation, panretinal photocoagulation for extensive ischemia (Eales, Behçet), focal laser for extrafoveal CNV, and anti-VEGF for subfoveal CNV or persistent NV." },
  { id: 15, question: "What is phacoantigenic uveitis and why does it matter for cataract timing?", answer: "A lens-induced (phacoantigenic/phacoanaphylactic) uveitis — an immune reaction to lens protein — which is an ABSOLUTE indication for cataract/lens removal; it cannot be controlled until the lens material is removed." },
  { id: 16, question: "Why are prostaglandin analogues used cautiously and miotics avoided in uveitic glaucoma?", answer: "Prostaglandin analogues can theoretically aggravate intraocular inflammation and cystoid macular edema, so they are used with caution. Miotics (cholinergics) worsen inflammation, increase the blood-aqueous barrier breakdown, and promote posterior synechiae — so they are contraindicated." },
];

const UVEITIS_COMPLIC_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "The most common complication of chronic uveitis is:", options: ["Band keratopathy", "Cataract (posterior subcapsular)", "Hypotony", "Scleritis"], correct: 1, explanation: "PSC cataract is the most common uveitis complication and the most common indication for surgery (up to 40% of vision loss)." },
  { id: 2, question: "Band keratopathy deposits calcium in the:", options: ["Corneal endothelium", "Interpalpebral epithelial basement membrane and Bowman layer", "Deep stroma", "Descemet membrane"], correct: 1, explanation: "Band keratopathy is interpalpebral calcific deposition in the epithelial BM/Bowman layer, with a limbal lucid interval and Swiss-cheese holes." },
  { id: 3, question: "A chelation agent used to treat band keratopathy is:", options: ["EDTA", "Pilocarpine", "Atropine", "Acetazolamide"], correct: 0, explanation: "EDTA 0.05 mol/L chelates calcium; debridement/keratectomy and PTK are alternatives." },
  { id: 4, question: "Before cataract surgery in uveitis, the eye should be quiescent for at least:", options: ["1 week", "3 months", "1 year", "No requirement"], correct: 1, explanation: "At least 3 months of quiescence (and good peri-operative control) reduces post-op inflammatory complications." },
  { id: 5, question: "Which IOL approach is preferred in a uveitic eye?", options: ["AC IOL", "Silicone IOL in the sulcus", "Single-piece acrylic PCIOL in the bag", "Leave aphakic always"], correct: 2, explanation: "A single-piece acrylic PCIOL in the capsular bag is preferred; avoid silicone, sulcus, and AC IOLs; defer IOL if poorly controlled." },
  { id: 6, question: "Steroid-induced IOP elevation typically begins:", options: ["Within hours", "2–6 weeks after starting steroids", "After 1 year only", "Never in uveitis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Steroid response occurs in ~1/3 of patients, usually 2–6 weeks in; risk higher in POAG, RA, diabetes, and age extremes." },
  { id: 7, question: "Which class of IOP-lowering drug is CONTRAINDICATED in uveitic glaucoma?", options: ["Beta-blockers", "Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors", "Miotics (cholinergics)", "Alpha-agonists"], correct: 2, explanation: "Miotics worsen inflammation and synechiae; prostaglandins are used with caution. First-line are BB, CAI, and alpha-agonists." },
  { id: 8, question: "Acute uveitic angle closure from posterior synechiae and iris bombé is a form of:", options: ["Pupillary-block angle closure", "Open-angle glaucoma", "Steroid response", "Hypotony"], correct: 0, explanation: "Seclusio pupillae → iris bombé → pupillary-block angle closure; treat with iridotomy plus inflammation control." },
  { id: 9, question: "Uveitic hypotony (IOP <6) in chronic disease results from:", options: ["Increased aqueous production", "Ciliary body atrophy / cyclitic membrane reducing aqueous", "Trabeculitis", "Steroid response"], correct: 1, explanation: "Chronic ciliary body shutdown/atrophy and cyclitic membranes cause hypotony, maculopathy, and risk of phthisis." },
  { id: 10, question: "Uveitic CME shows which FFA pattern?", options: ["Petalloid perifoveal leakage", "Blocked fluorescence", "Early hyperfluorescent dots", "Dark choroid"], correct: 0, explanation: "CME leaks from perifoveal capillaries into the OPL, giving a petalloid pattern on FFA." },
  { id: 11, question: "A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor helps CME by:", options: ["Blocking VEGF", "Reducing RPE pumping/leakage and aqueous production", "Dilating vessels", "Raising IOP"], correct: 1, explanation: "Acetazolamide reduces RPE fluid leakage (and aqueous production), an adjunct for uveitic CME." },
  { id: 12, question: "Exudative retinal detachment in uveitis is typical of:", options: ["VKH and sympathetic ophthalmia", "Viral retinitis", "Toxocariasis", "Eales disease"], correct: 0, explanation: "VKH, sympathetic ophthalmia, and uveal effusion cause exudative RD; viral retinitis → rhegmatogenous; toxocara/Eales → tractional." },
  { id: 13, question: "Rhegmatogenous RD complicating viral retinitis carries a high risk of:", options: ["Proliferative vitreoretinopathy (~30%)", "Band keratopathy", "Hypotony", "Cataract only"], correct: 0, explanation: "Necrotic retina in viral retinitis detaches with ~30% PVR risk and poor visual prognosis." },
  { id: 14, question: "Phacoantigenic (lens-induced) uveitis is:", options: ["A relative indication to defer surgery", "An ABSOLUTE indication for lens removal", "Treated with miotics", "Self-limiting"], correct: 1, explanation: "Lens-protein–driven inflammation cannot resolve until the lens material is removed — an absolute surgical indication." },
  { id: 15, question: "The main cytokines driving uveitic CME are:", options: ["VEGF and IL-6", "Insulin and glucagon", "Histamine only", "Complement C5 only"], correct: 0, explanation: "VEGF and IL-6 increase vascular permeability causing CME; NSAIDs/steroids/anti-VEGF target this." },
  { id: 16, question: "Panretinal photocoagulation in uveitis is indicated for:", options: ["Extensive retinal ischemia (e.g., Eales, Behçet)", "Band keratopathy", "Hypotony", "Cataract"], correct: 0, explanation: "PRP treats ischemia-driven retinal NV (Eales, Behçet); anti-VEGF/focal laser address CNV." },
];

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// UVEITIS · APPROACH & MEDICATIONS (CMU · "All About Uveitis")
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

const UVEITIS_APPROACH_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "What is the stepwise approach to a uveitis patient?", answer: "(1) Quick history taking; (2) targeted eye examination; (3) categorize the disease and build a differential; (4) review of systemic history and physical exam; (5) customized labs/investigations; (6) appropriate treatment and follow-up." },
  { id: 2, question: "How is uveitis categorized (SUN-based descriptors)?", answer: "By anatomy — anterior, intermediate, posterior, or panuveitis; by course — acute, chronic, or recurrent; by laterality — unilateral or bilateral; and by character — granulomatous or non-granulomatous. These descriptors drive the differential." },
  { id: 3, question: "What keratic-precipitate features help narrow the diagnosis?", answer: "Number (few vs heavy), distribution (diffuse, localized, linear), size (small/medium/large), and morphology (mutton-fat = granulomatous; stellate = Fuchs/viral; pigmented; coin-shaped/nodular/opalescent = CMV). Mutton-fat KPs indicate granulomatous disease (sarcoid, VKH, TB, sympathetic ophthalmia)." },
  { id: 4, question: "What basic ('pre-steroid') labs are sent in a uveitis work-up?", answer: "CBC, ESR, CRP; FBS, AST/ALT, BUN/Cr; anti-HIV, anti-HCV, HBsAg, VDRL, TPHA; chest X-ray; stool exam; urinalysis — then specific tests guided by the differential (e.g., ACE for sarcoid, aqueous PCR, skin test/IGRA for TB)." },
  { id: 5, question: "Summarize the cellular mechanism of corticosteroids in uveitis.", answer: "They reduce neutrophil migration, cause lymphocyte redistribution/lymphocytopenia (↓ antibody production), impair macrophage antigen presentation/bactericidal activity, stabilize mast cells, inhibit cytokine transcription (IL-1, TNF-α), block phospholipase A2 (↓ prostaglandins), reduce capillary permeability/vasodilation, and inhibit matrix metalloproteinases (↓ blood-retinal barrier breakdown)." },
  { id: 6, question: "Rank the anti-inflammatory potency of common corticosteroids.", answer: "Relative anti-inflammatory potency (systemic equivalent dose): hydrocortisone 1 (20 mg), prednisolone/prednisone 4 (5 mg), methylprednisolone/triamcinolone 5 (4 mg), dexamethasone 26 (0.75 mg), betamethasone 33 (0.6 mg)." },
  { id: 7, question: "When are topical vs periocular corticosteroids used, and what are the cautions?", answer: "Topical steroids are first-line for ANTERIOR uveitis (caution in herpetic keratitis/compromised cornea; taper slowly to avoid rebound). Periocular (sub-Tenon or transseptal) steroids are for intermediate/posterior uveitis and CME, or non-compliant/topical-unresponsive patients — but NOT in infectious or necrotizing scleritis (risk of thinning/perforation)." },
  { id: 8, question: "Compare the Ozurdex and Retisert intravitreal implants.", answer: "Ozurdex: a biodegradable dexamethasone 0.7 mg implant lasting ~6 months, FDA-approved for non-infectious posterior/panuveitis (and RVO/DME). Retisert: a non-biodegradable fluocinolone acetonide 0.59 mg implant releasing drug for ~30 months, FDA-approved for chronic non-infectious posterior uveitis — but causes cataract in nearly all phakic eyes and glaucoma in ~75%." },
  { id: 9, question: "Give a standard oral prednisolone regimen and taper for uveitis.", answer: "Loading 1–2 mg/kg/day, then gradual taper every 1–2 weeks (e.g., >40 mg: −10 mg; 40–20 mg: −5 mg; 20–10 mg: −2.5 mg; 10–0 mg: −1–2.5 mg every 1–4 weeks). If control requires >7.5–10 mg/day long-term, add steroid-sparing immunosuppressive therapy." },
  { id: 10, question: "When is pulse IV corticosteroid used?", answer: "For an explosive onset of severe non-infectious posterior uveitis or panuveitis needing immediate control: methylprednisolone 1 g/day over 60 minutes for 3 days, then switch to oral prednisolone 1–1.5 mg/kg/day (watch for numerous systemic side effects)." },
  { id: 11, question: "List the ocular side effects of corticosteroids.", answer: "Posterior subcapsular cataract, glaucoma/raised IOP, mydriasis, ptosis, susceptibility to infection, punctate keratopathy, and central serous chorioretinopathy (plus corneal/scleral thinning and delayed wound healing with topical use)." },
  { id: 12, question: "What monitoring is needed before and during long-term corticosteroids?", answer: "Before: complete systemic evaluation (infection, peptic ulcer, osteoporosis, DM/HT/CHF/CKD/psychiatric disease) with CBC, glucose, creatinine, anti-HIV/HCV, HBsAg, VDRL/TPHA, stool, UA, CXR. During: monitor blood sugar/BP, lipids yearly, bone densitometry (3 months then yearly); supplement calcium 1500 mg/day and vitamin D." },
  { id: 13, question: "What factors favor LOCAL versus SYSTEMIC treatment of uveitis?", answer: "Local (periocular/intravitreal) is preferred for unilateral chronic uveitis, children (steroids affect growth), and uncontrolled diabetes. Systemic is preferred for bilateral/sight-threatening uveitis, an underlying systemic disease requiring therapy, and uncontrolled glaucoma (to avoid steroid-response IOP rise from local depots)." },
  { id: 14, question: "Name the classes of immunosuppressive agents used in uveitis.", answer: "Antimetabolites (methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate), T-cell inhibitors/calcineurin inhibitors (ciclosporin, tacrolimus), alkylating agents (cyclophosphamide, chlorambucil), and biologic agents (anti-TNF such as adalimumab/infliximab). Adalimumab is FDA-approved for non-infectious intermediate/posterior/panuveitis." },
  { id: 15, question: "When is steroid-sparing immunosuppression added in uveitis?", answer: "When disease control requires long-term prednisolone above ~7.5–10 mg/day, when there is steroid intolerance/dependence, or for sight-threatening bilateral/posterior disease — to achieve durable control while minimizing corticosteroid toxicity." },
  { id: 16, question: "What are the principles ('hit hard, taper as art') of uveitis treatment?", answer: "Treat infection first if present, then suppress inflammation promptly and adequately ('hit hard, fast, adequate'), control IOP and complications, and taper corticosteroids slowly ('the art of tapering') to prevent rebound — individualizing local vs systemic therapy and adding immunosuppression when steroid demand stays high." },
];

const UVEITIS_APPROACH_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "Mutton-fat keratic precipitates indicate:", options: ["Non-granulomatous uveitis", "Granulomatous uveitis (sarcoid, VKH, TB, SO)", "Viral keratitis", "Episcleritis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Large greasy 'mutton-fat' KPs signify granulomatous inflammation; stellate KPs suggest Fuchs/viral." },
  { id: 2, question: "Which is part of the basic 'pre-steroid' uveitis lab panel?", options: ["VDRL/TPHA and CXR", "Brain MRI", "Echocardiogram", "Bone marrow biopsy"], correct: 0, explanation: "Baseline screening includes CBC/ESR/CRP, LFT/renal, anti-HIV/HCV/HBsAg, VDRL/TPHA, CXR, stool, and UA before steroids." },
  { id: 3, question: "The most potent anti-inflammatory corticosteroid listed (highest relative potency) is:", options: ["Prednisolone", "Hydrocortisone", "Betamethasone", "Triamcinolone"], correct: 2, explanation: "Betamethasone (potency ~33) and dexamethasone (~26) are the most potent; prednisolone is 4, hydrocortisone 1." },
  { id: 4, question: "First-line therapy for isolated anterior uveitis is:", options: ["Oral steroids", "Topical corticosteroids (+ cycloplegic)", "Intravitreal implant", "Immunosuppressives"], correct: 1, explanation: "Topical steroids (with a cycloplegic) are first-line for anterior uveitis; taper slowly to avoid rebound." },
  { id: 5, question: "Periocular steroid injection is CONTRAINDICATED in:", options: ["Intermediate uveitis", "Cystoid macular edema", "Infectious or necrotizing scleritis", "Non-compliant patients"], correct: 2, explanation: "Depot steroid risks thinning/perforation in necrotizing/infectious scleritis; it is used for IU/posterior uveitis and CME." },
  { id: 6, question: "The Ozurdex implant contains and lasts:", options: ["Fluocinolone, 30 months", "Dexamethasone 0.7 mg, ~6 months", "Triamcinolone, 3 years", "Prednisolone, 1 month"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ozurdex is a biodegradable dexamethasone 0.7 mg implant (~6 months) for non-infectious posterior/panuveitis." },
  { id: 7, question: "The Retisert (fluocinolone) implant characteristically causes:", options: ["Cataract in nearly all and glaucoma in ~75%", "No ocular side effects", "Corneal melt", "Hypotony only"], correct: 0, explanation: "The non-biodegradable fluocinolone implant lasts ~30 months but causes cataract in nearly all phakic eyes and glaucoma in ~75%." },
  { id: 8, question: "Long-term oral prednisolone requiring more than ~7.5–10 mg/day should prompt:", options: ["Stopping all treatment", "Adding steroid-sparing immunosuppression", "Doubling the steroid indefinitely", "Topical therapy only"], correct: 1, explanation: "A steroid requirement above ~7.5 mg/day long-term is the trigger to add immunosuppressive (steroid-sparing) therapy." },
  { id: 9, question: "Pulse IV methylprednisolone for severe panuveitis is dosed:", options: ["100 mg once", "1 g/day over 60 min for 3 days then oral", "4 g bolus then stop", "0.5 mg/kg/day"], correct: 1, explanation: "Explosive sight-threatening posterior/panuveitis: methylprednisolone 1 g/day ×3 days then oral prednisolone 1–1.5 mg/kg/day." },
  { id: 10, question: "A classic ocular side effect of corticosteroids is:", options: ["Posterior subcapsular cataract and glaucoma", "Keratoconus", "Retinitis pigmentosa", "Optic neuritis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Steroids cause PSC cataract, raised IOP/glaucoma, infection risk, and central serous chorioretinopathy." },
  { id: 11, question: "Before starting long-term corticosteroids you should screen for:", options: ["Infection, peptic ulcer, osteoporosis, diabetes", "Refractive error", "Color blindness", "Astigmatism"], correct: 0, explanation: "A full systemic evaluation (infection, PUD, osteoporosis, DM/HT/CKD/psychiatric) with baseline labs/CXR is required." },
  { id: 12, question: "Local (rather than systemic) therapy is preferred for:", options: ["Bilateral sight-threatening uveitis", "Unilateral chronic uveitis, children, uncontrolled diabetes", "Systemic disease requiring therapy", "Uncontrolled glaucoma"], correct: 1, explanation: "Local therapy suits unilateral chronic disease, children (growth), and uncontrolled DM; systemic is for bilateral/systemic disease." },
  { id: 13, question: "Which biologic is FDA-approved for non-infectious intermediate/posterior/panuveitis?", options: ["Rituximab", "Adalimumab", "Eculizumab", "Tocilizumab"], correct: 1, explanation: "Adalimumab (anti-TNF-α) is FDA-approved for non-infectious intermediate, posterior, and panuveitis." },
  { id: 14, question: "Corticosteroids block prostaglandin synthesis by inhibiting:", options: ["Cyclooxygenase directly", "Phospholipase A2 (arachidonic acid release)", "Lipoxygenase only", "Thromboxane synthase"], correct: 1, explanation: "Steroids inhibit phospholipase A2, cutting arachidonic acid release and all downstream eicosanoids." },
  { id: 15, question: "Which immunosuppressive is an ANTIMETABOLITE used in uveitis?", options: ["Ciclosporin", "Cyclophosphamide", "Methotrexate / mycophenolate", "Adalimumab"], correct: 2, explanation: "Methotrexate, azathioprine, and mycophenolate are antimetabolites; ciclosporin/tacrolimus are T-cell inhibitors; cyclophosphamide is alkylating." },
  { id: 16, question: "The overall treatment principle in active sight-threatening uveitis is:", options: ["Start low and go slow", "Hit hard/fast/adequate, then taper as an art", "Topical only regardless of severity", "Avoid steroids"], correct: 1, explanation: "Suppress inflammation promptly and adequately, then taper slowly to prevent rebound, individualizing local vs systemic therapy." },
];

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// UVEITIS · ANTERIOR & INTERMEDIATE — core non-viral causes + SUN framework
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

const UVEITIS_ANTINT_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "How does the SUN system classify uveitis by anatomic location?", answer: "By the PRIMARY site of inflammation: anterior (iris/ciliary body — iritis/iridocyclitis), intermediate (vitreous — with snowballs/snowbanks), posterior (retina/choroid), and panuveitis (all compartments). Also described by onset (sudden/insidious), duration (limited <3 months / persistent), and course (acute, recurrent, chronic)." },
  { id: 2, question: "State the SUN grading of anterior chamber CELLS (in a 1 mm × 1 mm slit beam).", answer: "0.5+ = 1–5 cells; 1+ = 6–15; 2+ = 16–25; 3+ = 26–50; 4+ = >50 cells. (AC flare is graded separately 0 to 4+: faint, moderate, marked, intense/fibrinous.) Cells grade activity; flare reflects protein and may persist after cells resolve." },
  { id: 3, question: "Describe HLA-B27-associated anterior uveitis.", answer: "The most common identifiable cause of acute anterior uveitis: acute, recurrent, UNILATERAL (or alternating), NON-granulomatous iridocyclitis, often with fibrin/plasmoid aqueous and sometimes a hypopyon; typically young adult males. It is associated with the seronegative spondyloarthropathies." },
  { id: 4, question: "List the spondyloarthropathies associated with HLA-B27 uveitis.", answer: "Ankylosing spondylitis (most common), reactive arthritis (formerly Reiter — the triad of urethritis, arthritis, and conjunctivitis/uveitis), psoriatic arthritis, and inflammatory bowel disease-associated (enteropathic) arthritis. Work-up: HLA-B27, sacroiliac imaging, and a review of back/joint/GI/skin symptoms." },
  { id: 5, question: "Describe juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA)-associated uveitis and why screening matters.", answer: "A CHRONIC, bilateral, NON-granulomatous anterior uveitis, classically in young girls who are ANA-positive, RF-negative, with oligoarticular JIA. The eye is characteristically WHITE and QUIET (asymptomatic), so it is detected only by scheduled slit-lamp screening — untreated it causes band keratopathy, posterior synechiae, cataract, glaucoma, and hypotony." },
  { id: 6, question: "How is JIA uveitis treated?", answer: "Topical corticosteroids and cycloplegics for flares, with early escalation to methotrexate and anti-TNF biologics (adalimumab) for chronic/steroid-dependent disease, coordinated with pediatric rheumatology; manage complications (band keratopathy, cataract, glaucoma)." },
  { id: 7, question: "What is intermediate uveitis / pars planitis, and its main cause of vision loss?", answer: "Inflammation primarily of the vitreous with snowballs (vitreous cellular aggregates) and a snowbank (white exudative plaque over the inferior pars plana). 'Pars planitis' is the idiopathic subset with snowbanking. Symptoms are floaters and blurred vision; CYSTOID MACULAR EDEMA is the leading cause of vision loss." },
  { id: 8, question: "What systemic diseases are associated with intermediate uveitis, and the treatment ladder?", answer: "Multiple sclerosis, sarcoidosis, Lyme disease, syphilis, and tuberculosis (work these up). The stepladder (Kaplan): observation if mild → periocular/regional corticosteroid → systemic corticosteroid/immunosuppression → pars plana vitrectomy or peripheral cryotherapy/laser for refractory disease." },
  { id: 9, question: "What is lens-induced (phacoantigenic) uveitis?", answer: "A granulomatous inflammatory reaction to exposed lens protein after capsule rupture (trauma/surgery) or a hypermature/leaky cataract. It can mimic endophthalmitis; definitive treatment is removal of the lens material (an absolute surgical indication)." },
  { id: 10, question: "Describe tubulointerstitial nephritis and uveitis (TINU) syndrome.", answer: "A bilateral, sudden-onset, non-granulomatous anterior (± intermediate) uveitis in adolescents (girls > boys) with acute tubulointerstitial nephritis — fatigue, weight loss, fever, flank pain, elevated creatinine, and raised urinary beta-2-microglobulin (± eosinophilia); renal biopsy confirms. The nephritis is often self-limited but the uveitis frequently recurs and needs steroids." },
  { id: 11, question: "Name important causes of DRUG-induced uveitis.", answer: "Rifabutin (hypopyon uveitis), cidofovir, IV bisphosphonates, systemic/topical moxifloxacin (bilateral AU with iris transillumination/pigment dispersion — 'BAIT'), brimonidine (granulomatous AU), sulfonamides, and immune checkpoint inhibitors (anti-PD-1/CTLA-4). Stopping or reducing the drug is key." },
  { id: 12, question: "Which masquerade syndromes mimic uveitis, by age?", answer: "Children: retinoblastoma, leukemia, and juvenile xanthogranuloma (spontaneous hyphema). Adults: primary vitreoretinal lymphoma (older patients, bilateral steroid-resistant vitritis — vitreous biopsy shows a high IL-10:IL-6 ratio), intraocular foreign body, and chronic rhegmatogenous retinal detachment. Suspect a masquerade when 'uveitis' fails to respond to appropriate therapy." },
  { id: 13, question: "What distinguishes GRANULOMATOUS from non-granulomatous anterior uveitis, and why does it matter?", answer: "Granulomatous AU has large greasy 'mutton-fat' KPs and iris (Koeppe/Busacca) nodules — suggesting sarcoidosis, TB, syphilis, VKH, or sympathetic ophthalmia. Non-granulomatous AU has fine KPs — suggesting HLA-B27, JIA, TINU, or idiopathic disease. The character steers the differential and work-up." },
  { id: 14, question: "How does the ONSET and LATERALITY of anterior uveitis narrow the differential?", answer: "Acute, unilateral, recurrent non-granulomatous AU → HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathy or herpetic. Chronic, bilateral, quiet AU → JIA (child) or sarcoid/TINU. Unilateral with high IOP and iris atrophy → herpetic/CMV. Bilateral granulomatous → sarcoid, TB, syphilis, VKH." },
  { id: 15, question: "Why must infectious anterior uveitis be excluded before aggressive steroid monotherapy?", answer: "Herpetic (HSV/VZV/CMV), syphilitic, and tubercular anterior uveitis can masquerade as idiopathic AU; unopposed corticosteroids can worsen infection. Features that flag infection: unilateral high IOP, iris atrophy/sectoral transillumination, corneal involvement, and poor/atypical response — prompting aqueous PCR and serology before/alongside treatment." },
  { id: 16, question: "What is the general first-line treatment of non-infectious anterior uveitis and the goal?", answer: "Topical corticosteroids (dosed to the degree of inflammation, tapered slowly to avoid rebound) plus a cycloplegic/mydriatic (to relieve ciliary spasm and prevent/break posterior synechiae). The goal is to fully quiet the AC (0 cells) while monitoring IOP (steroid response) and screening for the underlying systemic association." },
];

const UVEITIS_ANTINT_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "The SUN grade for 16–25 anterior chamber cells in a 1 mm beam is:", options: ["0.5+", "1+", "2+", "4+"], correct: 2, explanation: "SUN: 0.5+ =1–5, 1+ =6–15, 2+ =16–25, 3+ =26–50, 4+ =>50 cells." },
  { id: 2, question: "The most common identifiable cause of acute anterior uveitis is:", options: ["Sarcoidosis", "HLA-B27-associated disease", "JIA", "Toxoplasmosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "HLA-B27 (spondyloarthropathy-associated) acute, recurrent, unilateral non-granulomatous AU is the most common identifiable cause." },
  { id: 3, question: "A young man with recurrent unilateral painful red eye, fibrin, and a hypopyon most likely has:", options: ["JIA uveitis", "HLA-B27 anterior uveitis", "Fuchs uveitis", "Pars planitis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Acute unilateral non-granulomatous AU with fibrin/hypopyon in a young man is classic HLA-B27 disease (ankylosing spondylitis)." },
  { id: 4, question: "Reactive arthritis (formerly Reiter) classically includes:", options: ["Urethritis, arthritis, and conjunctivitis/uveitis", "Rash, fever, lymphadenopathy", "Deafness, teeth, keratitis", "Vitiligo, poliosis, alopecia"], correct: 0, explanation: "The triad is urethritis, arthritis, and ocular inflammation (conjunctivitis/uveitis) — a seronegative spondyloarthropathy." },
  { id: 5, question: "JIA-associated uveitis is characteristically:", options: ["Acute, painful, unilateral, granulomatous", "Chronic, bilateral, WHITE/quiet, non-granulomatous in ANA+ girls", "Associated with hypopyon", "Seen only in boys"], correct: 1, explanation: "JIA uveitis is a chronic, asymptomatic (white eye) bilateral non-granulomatous AU in young ANA-positive, RF-negative girls — detected by screening." },
  { id: 6, question: "Why is scheduled slit-lamp screening essential in JIA?", options: ["The eye is painful and obvious", "The eye is quiet/asymptomatic yet silently develops cataract, glaucoma, band keratopathy", "It screens for retinoblastoma", "It measures axial length"], correct: 1, explanation: "JIA uveitis is silent; without screening it progresses undetected to vision-threatening complications." },
  { id: 7, question: "A snowbank over the inferior pars plana with floaters and macular edema indicates:", options: ["Anterior uveitis", "Intermediate uveitis / pars planitis", "Posterior scleritis", "Acute retinal necrosis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Snowballs/snowbanking define intermediate uveitis; pars planitis is the idiopathic subset, with CME the main cause of vision loss." },
  { id: 8, question: "Which systemic disease is classically associated with intermediate uveitis?", options: ["Multiple sclerosis", "Rheumatoid arthritis", "Diabetes", "Hypertension"], correct: 0, explanation: "Intermediate uveitis is associated with MS, sarcoidosis, Lyme, syphilis, and TB — work these up." },
  { id: 9, question: "The leading cause of vision loss in intermediate uveitis is:", options: ["Cataract", "Cystoid macular edema", "Retinal detachment", "Glaucoma"], correct: 1, explanation: "CME is the principal cause of visual loss in intermediate uveitis; treatment targets it." },
  { id: 10, question: "A granulomatous lens-protein reaction after a ruptured cataract is:", options: ["Phacoantigenic (lens-induced) uveitis", "Sympathetic ophthalmia", "Endophthalmitis", "JIA uveitis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Lens-induced (phacoantigenic) uveitis reacts to exposed lens protein; definitive treatment is lens removal." },
  { id: 11, question: "An adolescent girl with bilateral sudden AU, fatigue, raised creatinine, and high urinary beta-2-microglobulin has:", options: ["JIA", "TINU syndrome", "Sarcoidosis", "HLA-B27 disease"], correct: 1, explanation: "Tubulointerstitial nephritis and uveitis (TINU): bilateral AU with acute interstitial nephritis (β2-microglobulinuria, raised creatinine)." },
  { id: 12, question: "Rifabutin and IV bisphosphonates are recognized causes of:", options: ["Drug-induced uveitis", "Cataract", "Band keratopathy", "Scleritis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Rifabutin (hypopyon AU), cidofovir, bisphosphonates, moxifloxacin, brimonidine, and checkpoint inhibitors cause drug-induced uveitis." },
  { id: 13, question: "Older patient with bilateral vitritis unresponsive to steroids — suspect:", options: ["Primary vitreoretinal lymphoma (masquerade)", "Pars planitis", "HLA-B27 uveitis", "Episcleritis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Steroid-resistant vitritis in an older patient suggests vitreoretinal lymphoma; vitreous biopsy shows a high IL-10:IL-6 ratio." },
  { id: 14, question: "Mutton-fat KPs and iris nodules indicate ___ uveitis.", options: ["Non-granulomatous", "Granulomatous", "Viral only", "Drug-induced"], correct: 1, explanation: "Mutton-fat KPs and Koeppe/Busacca nodules mark granulomatous disease (sarcoid, TB, syphilis, VKH, SO)." },
  { id: 15, question: "First-line therapy for acute non-infectious anterior uveitis is:", options: ["Topical steroid plus a cycloplegic", "Oral immunosuppression", "Intravitreal implant", "Laser"], correct: 0, explanation: "Topical steroids (tapered) with a cycloplegic to relieve spasm and prevent/break posterior synechiae are first-line." },
  { id: 16, question: "A masquerade syndrome to exclude in a CHILD with 'uveitis' and leukocoria is:", options: ["Retinoblastoma", "Pars planitis", "HLA-B27 uveitis", "TINU"], correct: 0, explanation: "Retinoblastoma (and leukemia/JXG) can masquerade as pediatric uveitis — a vision- and life-threatening miss." },
];

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// UVEITIS · CLINICAL CASES & APPROACH — vignette-based diagnostic reasoning
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Each recall card is a case worked through the same framework:
// CATEGORIZE (SUN) → DIFFERENTIAL → INVESTIGATIONS → DIAGNOSIS → next step.

const UVEITIS_CASES_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "CASE: A 28-year-old man presents with his third episode of a painful, red, photophobic RIGHT eye. Exam: AC 3+ cells, fibrin, a small hypopyon, fine (non-granulomatous) KPs, and early posterior synechiae; the left eye is normal. He mentions months of low back stiffness worse in the morning. Work through category → differential → investigations → diagnosis.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: acute, unilateral, recurrent, NON-granulomatous ANTERIOR uveitis (with fibrin/hypopyon). DIFFERENTIAL: HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathy, Behçet, idiopathic, herpetic. INVESTIGATIONS: HLA-B27, sacroiliac X-ray/MRI, review of back/GI/skin symptoms. DIAGNOSIS: HLA-B27-associated anterior uveitis with ankylosing spondylitis. NEXT STEP: topical steroid + cycloplegic; refer to rheumatology." },
  { id: 2, question: "CASE: A 5-year-old GIRL is referred from a screening clinic with NO ocular symptoms. Both eyes are white and quiet, but show band keratopathy, posterior synechiae, and 1+ AC cells. She has a painless swollen knee and is ANA-positive, RF-negative. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: chronic, bilateral, NON-granulomatous ANTERIOR uveitis in a WHITE/quiet eye. DIFFERENTIAL: JIA-associated uveitis (classic), TINU, sarcoid, idiopathic. INVESTIGATIONS: ANA, RF, pediatric rheumatology review, scheduled slit-lamp screening. DIAGNOSIS: JIA-associated chronic anterior uveitis. NEXT STEP: topical steroid + cycloplegic, early methotrexate/adalimumab; treat band keratopathy/cataract/glaucoma." },
  { id: 3, question: "CASE: A 45-year-old woman has bilateral blurred vision and floaters. Exam: mutton-fat KPs, Koeppe iris nodules, AC cells, vitritis, and 'candle-wax dripping' retinal periphlebitis. She has a chronic dry cough. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: chronic, bilateral, GRANULOMATOUS PANUVEITIS. DIFFERENTIAL: sarcoidosis, TB, syphilis, VKH, sympathetic ophthalmia. INVESTIGATIONS: CXR/HRCT (hilar adenopathy), serum ACE/lysozyme, VDRL/TPHA, IGRA/TST, ± tissue biopsy. DIAGNOSIS: ocular sarcoidosis (non-caseating granulomas). NEXT STEP: corticosteroids ± steroid-sparing immunosuppression; pulmonary referral." },
  { id: 4, question: "CASE: A 30-year-old man has unilateral floaters and blurred vision. Fundus shows a focal WHITE retinochoroiditis adjacent to a pigmented scar, with dense overlying vitritis ('headlight in the fog'). Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: unilateral POSTERIOR uveitis (focal retinochoroiditis with vitritis). DIFFERENTIAL: toxoplasmosis (classic), TB, syphilis, ARN. INVESTIGATIONS: usually CLINICAL; Toxoplasma IgG/IgM (IgG-negative argues against), aqueous PCR/Goldmann-Witmer if atypical. DIAGNOSIS: reactivated ocular toxoplasmosis. NEXT STEP: treat if vision-threatening — pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid (+ steroid started 48 h later)." },
  { id: 5, question: "CASE: A 35-year-old man with AIDS (CD4 15/µL) reports floaters and a peripheral field defect. Fundus shows granular and hemorrhagic retinal necrosis along the arcades with only mild vitritis. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: unilateral POSTERIOR uveitis — necrotizing retinitis with minimal vitritis in profound immunosuppression. DIFFERENTIAL: CMV retinitis (classic at CD4<50), ARN, toxoplasmosis. INVESTIGATIONS: CD4 count, aqueous CMV PCR if atypical. DIAGNOSIS: CMV retinitis. NEXT STEP: IV/oral ganciclovir or valganciclovir ± intravitreal ganciclovir, and restore immune function (antiretrovirals)." },
  { id: 6, question: "CASE: A 26-year-old man of Mediterranean descent has recurrent painful oral and genital ulcers and now bilateral blurred vision. Exam: a shifting (mobile) hypopyon and occlusive necrotizing retinal vasculitis. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: recurrent, bilateral, NON-granulomatous PANUVEITIS with mobile hypopyon and occlusive retinal vasculitis. DIFFERENTIAL: Behçet disease, HLA-B27 (anterior only), infectious vasculitis. INVESTIGATIONS: clinical criteria, pathergy test, HLA-B51, exclude infection. DIAGNOSIS: Behçet disease. NEXT STEP: systemic corticosteroid + immunomodulation (azathioprine first-line; infliximab/adalimumab)." },
  { id: 7, question: "CASE: A 40-year-old woman has bilateral sudden severe vision loss with headache and tinnitus. Exam: multifocal serous (exudative) retinal detachments and disc hyperemia; weeks later she develops poliosis and vitiligo. No history of ocular trauma/surgery. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: acute, bilateral, GRANULOMATOUS PANUVEITIS with exudative RDs and neuro/integumentary signs. DIFFERENTIAL: VKH, sympathetic ophthalmia (needs an injured eye), posterior scleritis, central serous. INVESTIGATIONS: OCT (serous RD), FA (pinpoint leaks/pooling), LP (CSF pleocytosis), and absence of trauma. DIAGNOSIS: Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada disease. NEXT STEP: prompt high-dose corticosteroids with slow taper + immunosuppression." },
  { id: 8, question: "CASE: A 55-year-old man has a unilateral red eye with IOP 40 mmHg, a few coin-shaped KPs, diffuse iris atrophy, and only low-grade AC cells. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: unilateral ANTERIOR uveitis with raised IOP out of proportion to inflammation. DIFFERENTIAL: CMV anterior uveitis/endotheliitis, herpetic (HSV/VZV) AU, Posner-Schlossman. INVESTIGATIONS: aqueous PCR (CMV/HSV/VZV), IOP/gonioscopy. DIAGNOSIS: CMV anterior uveitis. NEXT STEP: ganciclovir (systemic/topical) + topical steroid + IOP-lowering therapy (avoid miotics)." },
  { id: 9, question: "CASE: A 22-year-old healthy student has bilateral floaters and mildly reduced vision. Exam: vitreous snowballs, a white exudative snowbank over the inferior pars plana, and cystoid macular edema; the anterior chamber is nearly quiet. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: bilateral INTERMEDIATE uveitis (vitritis with snowballs/snowbank), CME-limited vision. DIFFERENTIAL: pars planitis (idiopathic), multiple sclerosis, sarcoidosis, Lyme, syphilis, TB. INVESTIGATIONS: MRI brain (MS), ACE/CXR, Lyme serology, RPR/TPHA, IGRA. DIAGNOSIS: pars planitis (after excluding associations). NEXT STEP: treat CME via the stepladder — periocular/regional steroid → systemic/immunosuppression → vitrectomy if refractory." },
  { id: 10, question: "CASE: A 50-year-old presents with unilateral decreased vision and a placoid yellow-grey lesion at the macula with mild AC/vitreous cells. He recalls a faint rash on the palms and soles. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: unilateral POSTERIOR/pan uveitis — a placoid outer-retinal/RPE lesion. DIFFERENTIAL: syphilis (ASPPC — pathognomonic of secondary syphilis), APMPPE, TB, sarcoid. INVESTIGATIONS: VDRL/RPR (activity) + FTA-ABS/TPHA (marker), HIV, FAF (hyperautofluorescent)/ICG (confluent hypofluorescence), LP. DIAGNOSIS: ocular syphilis (ASPPC). NEXT STEP: treat as neurosyphilis — IV aqueous penicillin G." },
  { id: 11, question: "CASE: A 62-year-old has bilateral floaters and declining vision with dense vitritis that keeps RECURRING despite repeated corticosteroids; there are creamy sub-RPE infiltrates, and he reports subtle memory/behaviour change. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: bilateral, chronic, STEROID-RESISTANT vitritis in an older patient — a masquerade. DIFFERENTIAL: primary vitreoretinal (CNS) lymphoma, infectious retinitis, sarcoid. INVESTIGATIONS: diagnostic vitrectomy/biopsy (cytology, IL-10:IL-6 ratio >1), MRI brain, LP. DIAGNOSIS: primary vitreoretinal lymphoma. NEXT STEP: neuro-oncology referral; intravitreal methotrexate/rituximab ± systemic/CNS therapy — do NOT simply escalate steroids." },
  { id: 12, question: "CASE: A 2-year-old has a white pupil (leukocoria) in one eye and what looks like a 'pseudo-hypopyon'; the eye is NOT painful or red. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: a pediatric uveitis MASQUERADE (leukocoria + pseudohypopyon). DIFFERENTIAL: retinoblastoma (must exclude first), Coats disease, toxocariasis, persistent fetal vasculature. INVESTIGATIONS: examination under anesthesia, B-scan (intralesional calcification), MRI orbit/brain — do NOT biopsy an intraocular mass if retinoblastoma is possible. DIAGNOSIS: retinoblastoma until proven otherwise. NEXT STEP: urgent ocular oncology referral." },
];

const UVEITIS_CASES_MCQ: MCQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "A 25-year-old man has recurrent acute unilateral non-granulomatous iritis with fibrin and low back pain. The single most useful test is:", options: ["Serum ACE", "HLA-B27 and sacroiliac imaging", "Toxoplasma serology", "Vitreous biopsy"], correct: 1, explanation: "This is HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathy-associated AU; HLA-B27 and SI joint imaging confirm the association." },
  { id: 2, question: "A quiet white eye in an ANA-positive young girl with band keratopathy is typical of:", options: ["HLA-B27 uveitis", "JIA-associated uveitis", "Pars planitis", "Behçet"], correct: 1, explanation: "Chronic asymptomatic (white) bilateral AU in ANA-positive girls is JIA uveitis — found only by screening." },
  { id: 3, question: "Bilateral granulomatous panuveitis with mutton-fat KPs, candle-wax periphlebitis, and hilar adenopathy on CXR is:", options: ["Sarcoidosis", "HLA-B27 disease", "Toxoplasmosis", "Pars planitis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Granulomatous panuveitis with candle-wax drippings and hilar adenopathy indicates ocular sarcoidosis." },
  { id: 4, question: "'Headlight in the fog' (focal retinochoroiditis beside a pigmented scar) is:", options: ["CMV retinitis", "Toxoplasmic retinochoroiditis", "ARN", "Syphilitic ASPPC"], correct: 1, explanation: "Dense vitritis over a focal white lesion next to an old scar is reactivated toxoplasmosis." },
  { id: 5, question: "A patient with CD4 12/µL and granular hemorrhagic retinitis with minimal vitritis most likely has:", options: ["ARN", "CMV retinitis", "Toxoplasmosis", "Behçet"], correct: 1, explanation: "Low CD4 with indolent granular/hemorrhagic retinitis and little vitritis is CMV retinitis." },
  { id: 6, question: "Recurrent oral/genital ulcers with a MOBILE hypopyon and occlusive retinal vasculitis indicate:", options: ["Sarcoidosis", "Behçet disease", "VKH", "HLA-B27 uveitis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Mobile hypopyon + occlusive retinal vasculitis + aphthous/genital ulcers define Behçet disease." },
  { id: 7, question: "Bilateral exudative retinal detachments with poliosis, tinnitus, and CSF pleocytosis (no ocular trauma) is:", options: ["Sympathetic ophthalmia", "Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada disease", "Posterior scleritis", "Central serous"], correct: 1, explanation: "VKH: bilateral granulomatous panuveitis with serous RDs and neuro/auditory/integumentary signs, without a history of eye injury." },
  { id: 8, question: "Unilateral AU with IOP 40, coin-shaped KPs, and diffuse iris atrophy — the best confirmatory test is:", options: ["Serum IgG", "Aqueous PCR for CMV", "Chest CT", "HLA-B27"], correct: 1, explanation: "This CMV anterior uveitis/endotheliitis picture is confirmed by aqueous CMV PCR." },
  { id: 9, question: "Snowballs and an inferior snowbank with CME define:", options: ["Anterior uveitis", "Intermediate uveitis", "Posterior scleritis", "Panuveitis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Vitreous snowballs/snowbanking are the hallmark of intermediate uveitis; CME drives vision loss." },
  { id: 10, question: "A young adult with new intermediate uveitis should be screened for which systemic disease with brain MRI?", options: ["Multiple sclerosis", "Rheumatoid arthritis", "Diabetes", "Psoriasis"], correct: 0, explanation: "Intermediate uveitis is associated with MS; brain MRI (plus sarcoid/Lyme/syphilis work-up) is appropriate." },
  { id: 11, question: "A placoid yellow macular lesion with a palm/sole rash — the key tests are:", options: ["VDRL/RPR plus FTA-ABS/TPHA (and HIV)", "ACE only", "Toxoplasma IgG", "HLA-B51"], correct: 0, explanation: "ASPPC is pathognomonic of secondary syphilis; confirm with treponemal and non-treponemal tests and screen HIV." },
  { id: 12, question: "Steroid-RESISTANT bilateral vitritis in a 65-year-old with CNS symptoms warrants:", options: ["Higher-dose steroids", "Diagnostic vitrectomy (IL-10:IL-6, cytology) and brain MRI", "Topical therapy", "Observation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Suspect primary vitreoretinal lymphoma — biopsy (IL-10:IL-6 >1) and neuroimaging rather than escalating steroids." },
  { id: 13, question: "The first masquerade to exclude in a young child with leukocoria and 'pseudohypopyon' is:", options: ["Pars planitis", "Retinoblastoma", "JIA uveitis", "Toxocariasis"], correct: 1, explanation: "Retinoblastoma must be excluded first (EUA, B-scan for calcification, MRI) — never biopsy a possible retinoblastoma." },
  { id: 14, question: "An HIV-negative patient's acute anterior uveitis with sectoral iris atrophy and raised IOP suggests:", options: ["Herpetic (HSV/VZV) anterior uveitis", "HLA-B27 disease", "JIA", "TINU"], correct: 0, explanation: "Unilateral AU with sectoral iris atrophy and high IOP (trabeculitis) is herpetic; aqueous PCR and antivirals apply." },
  { id: 15, question: "An adolescent girl with bilateral AU, fatigue, and abnormal renal function should be tested for:", options: ["Urinary beta-2-microglobulin / serum creatinine (TINU)", "HLA-B27", "Toxoplasma PCR", "ACE"], correct: 0, explanation: "TINU: bilateral AU with tubulointerstitial nephritis — check creatinine and urinary β2-microglobulin (± eosinophilia)." },
  { id: 16, question: "The single best FIRST question that organizes any uveitis differential is:", options: ["Is the patient HLA-B27 positive?", "What is the primary ANATOMIC location (anterior/intermediate/posterior/pan)?", "What is the IOP?", "Is there a cataract?"], correct: 1, explanation: "Anatomic localization (SUN) plus laterality, course, and granulomatous character frames the entire differential and work-up." },
];

// ── Uveitis regrouped into 3 anatomical books (parts flattened per book) ──
// The two mixed decks are split: non-infectious → scleritis (anterior) vs
// posterior/pan; viral → anterior vs posterior. Infectious-posterior keeps
// only its bacterial cards (syphilis/TB/bartonella); toxoplasmosis is covered
// by the comprehensive BCSC toxo deck (UVEITIS_TOXO).
