// ── Uveitis: Complications & Management · cases
const UV_COMP_CASES_RECALL: RecallQ[] = [
  { id: 1, question: "CASE: A patient with chronic uveitis has gradually reduced central vision; the eye is quiet, but OCT shows intraretinal cysts and FFA shows a petalloid perifoveal leakage pattern. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: uveitic cystoid macular edema (a leading cause of vision loss in uveitis). INVESTIGATIONS: OCT (cysts), FFA (petalloid leakage). NEXT STEP: control the underlying inflammation, then target CME — NSAIDs, regional/intravitreal steroid (triamcinolone/implant), acetazolamide, anti-VEGF, and steroid-sparing immunosuppression." },
  { id: 2, question: "CASE: A uveitis patient on topical prednisolone develops an IOP of 32 mmHg 3 weeks after starting treatment, with an open angle and quiet inflammation. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: likely corticosteroid-induced ocular hypertension (onset 2–6 weeks, ~1/3 of patients; steroid responders include POAG/diabetics). DIFFERENTIAL: steroid response vs active inflammatory/trabeculitic IOP rise. NEXT STEP: lower/replace the steroid if inflammation allows, add IOP-lowering therapy (beta-blocker/CAI/alpha-agonist — avoid miotics), and monitor." },
  { id: 3, question: "CASE: A uveitis patient has a visually significant cataract. When and how should surgery be done?", answer: "CATEGORIZE: uveitic cataract (PSC; the commonest surgical complication). TIMING: the eye must be quiescent for ≥3 months with good peri-operative control. TECHNIQUE: pre-treat with steroids (± antivirals for herpetic eyes), a single-piece acrylic PCIOL in the bag, avoid silicone/sulcus/AC IOLs; defer IOL if poorly controlled. NEXT STEP: aggressive post-op anti-inflammatory therapy." },
  { id: 4, question: "CASE: A chronic uveitis patient has an IOP of 4 mmHg with reduced vision, choroidal folds, and a cyclitic membrane. Work through the approach.", answer: "CATEGORIZE: uveitic HYPOTONY (IOP <6) from ciliary body shutdown/atrophy and a cyclitic membrane. DIFFERENTIAL: acute (inflammatory CB shutdown) vs chronic (atrophy/traction). INVESTIGATIONS: OCT (hypotony maculopathy), ultrasound. NEXT STEP: control inflammation (steroids); surgical membrane removal/PPV in select cases; prognosis is often poor (risk of phthisis)." },
];
